WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.780 --> 00:00:18.180
Tim Ferriss: Hello, boys and girls, ladies&nbsp;
and germs. This is Tim Ferriss. Welcome to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:22.680
another episode of The Tim Ferriss Show, where&nbsp;
it is always my job to deconstruct world-class&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:26.940
performers, to interview people who are very good&nbsp;
at what they do, to find out what makes them tick,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:31.620
their influences, things you can use, that you&nbsp;
can borrow from their life, from their lived&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:36.540
experience. And my guest today is David Eagleman.&nbsp;
You can find him on Twitter @DavidEagleman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.540 --> 00:00:41.460
Eagle Man, just as you would think it's&nbsp;
spelled. And David is a neuroscientist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:46.140
New York Times bestselling author, TED speaker,&nbsp;
and Guggenheim Fellow. We're going to come back to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.140 --> 00:00:50.760
the Guggenheim Fellow, I think, very shortly. He&nbsp;
is the writer and presenter of the Emmy-nominated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:56.160
series The Brain on PBS, as well as the podcast&nbsp;
Inner Cosmos with David Eagleman. In Palo Alto,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.160 --> 00:01:00.300
California, he teaches at Stanford University,&nbsp;
you may have heard of it. Runs a startup neurotech&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:06.180
company called Neosensory and directs the Center&nbsp;
for Science and Law. Dr. Eagleman also runs a film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:10.800
and television production company, Cognito&nbsp;
Entertainment, to bring scientific themes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:15.960
fiction, and non-fiction to the screen. And in&nbsp;
his spare time, he is the author of eight books,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:21.420
including the international bestsellers Sum,&nbsp;
Incognito — I learn. I learn — and his newest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.420 --> 00:01:27.480
book, Livewired. You can find all things David&nbsp;
at eagleman.com. David, it’s so nice to finally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:31.307
carve out the time to have this conversation.
David Eagleman: Great. Great to be here, Tim.&nbsp;

00:01:31.307 --> 00:01:36.900
Tim Ferriss: And I thought we would start&nbsp;
with a topic while near and dear to my heart,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:42.060
it's not something I know that much about and that&nbsp;
is synesthesia. And I'll preface that by saying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.660 --> 00:01:49.320
one book that I found somehow, I don't know&nbsp;
how, in high school, which later led to me for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:54.000
a very brief stint being a neuroscience major or&nbsp;
a psychology major with a focus on neuroscience,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.000 --> 00:02:01.124
was a book by A.R. Luria. And I may screw&nbsp;
up another word here, but The Mind of a —&nbsp;

00:02:01.124 --> 00:02:04.140
David Eagleman: Mnemonist.
Tim Ferriss: Mnemonist. I always&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:12.360
want to say Nemonist, but like mnemonic device,&nbsp;
Mnemosyne, the Greek goddess of memory. So that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:21.240
gave me insight as any reader of this book would&nbsp;
find into a vast memory and what maybe hypermnesia&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:28.980
or incredibly unusual performance in that arena&nbsp;
can look like. Would you mind just taking a moment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.980 --> 00:02:36.900
to define synesthesia and to then describe the&nbsp;
work that you've done in that area or aspects&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:40.680
of it that you find particularly interesting?
David Eagleman: So a mnemonist is someone who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:48.000
can memorize anything in their life, as you know&nbsp;
from the book, giant list of arbitrary numbers or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:51.720
things like that. Some people have this ability&nbsp;
and it turns out, as far as I can tell anyway,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:57.300
that every single one of these people has&nbsp;
synesthesia. So synesthesia is where there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.300 --> 00:03:03.960
an association between some stimulus and something&nbsp;
else. So for example, numbers triggering a color&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:09.600
experience. So three is purple, nine is yellow,&nbsp;
and so on. But it's not only colors, it can be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:17.400
other things like gender and personality and shape&nbsp;
and size and things like that. So for someone who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:22.620
has synesthesia, when they're memorizing,&nbsp;
let's say the digits of Pi, there's a whole&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.620 --> 00:03:31.860
story landscape going on where the small yellow&nbsp;
girl who is number three is talking to the angry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.860 --> 00:03:37.740
boy who's number seven, and then who's talking&nbsp;
to the old woman who's number nine and so on.&nbsp;

00:03:37.740 --> 00:03:45.000
There's a whole story there, and that's how&nbsp;
mnemonists tend to memorize their stuff. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:48.780
synesthesia, it turns out, is quite common.&nbsp;
It's about three percent of the population has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.780 --> 00:03:52.740
some form of synesthesia. And the thing&nbsp;
I've always found fascinating about it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.740 --> 00:03:57.960
the reason I've studied it for about 20 years&nbsp;
now, is because it is just an example of how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.960 --> 00:04:02.400
reality can be different in your head and in&nbsp;
my head. And we would both accept it as okay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:07.020
that's how the world is. I should just mention&nbsp;
there are all these forms of synesthesia like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.740 --> 00:04:11.820
hearing might lead to a taste in your mouth&nbsp;
or tasting something might lead to a feeling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.820 --> 00:04:16.920
on your fingertips or a loud sound might cause&nbsp;
you to see something or whatever. It's just any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.920 --> 00:04:22.980
blending of the senses, we call that synesthesia.
Tim Ferriss: All right. Many follow up questions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.980 --> 00:04:26.040
because I've never had a conversation&nbsp;
with someone who has spent the amount of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.040 --> 00:04:32.280
time looking at synesthesia as you have. So two&nbsp;
immediate questions come to mind. So mnemonist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:39.660
would you say that that label applies&nbsp;
to only those who seem to organically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.660 --> 00:04:44.880
have these abilities and maybe the presence of&nbsp;
synesthesia? Or does it also apply to people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.880 --> 00:04:51.180
who develop or learn techniques for, say,&nbsp;
competing in memory competitions? Because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.180 --> 00:04:57.960
they can do things that are seemingly&nbsp;
beyond the reach of the average human,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.960 --> 00:05:04.080
but they're not Kim Peek reading two books at the&nbsp;
same time, one eye on each page, let's just say.&nbsp;

00:05:04.080 --> 00:05:08.160
David Eagleman: So like with&nbsp;
many things, there's congenital,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.600 --> 00:05:15.360
which means you're born with it or acquired, which&nbsp;
means you get it some other way. So for example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.960 --> 00:05:21.420
there's congenital synesthesia where you're born&nbsp;
with this mixture of the senses, but acquired is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.420 --> 00:05:26.100
when people take certain psychedelic drugs&nbsp;
and they listen to music and it cause them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.100 --> 00:05:31.140
to see colors and shapes, that sort of thing.&nbsp;
So yes, you could be a mnemonist that acquires&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.140 --> 00:05:35.400
your skills through hard training, and usually&nbsp;
that training involves just tapping into other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.060 --> 00:05:41.640
modalities like we're really good at location.&nbsp;
All animals really care a lot about locations.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.640 --> 00:05:45.600
So you know all the stuff about memory palaces&nbsp;
and whatever. And you're memorizing a sequence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.600 --> 00:05:51.180
by placing things in your mind in different&nbsp;
locations in your house, let's say. So that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.180 --> 00:05:54.900
the way that somebody trains is by tapping in.&nbsp;
And really that's what synesthesia is about,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.900 --> 00:06:01.020
is neighboring areas of the brain that are sort of&nbsp;
tied together more than you would normally expect.&nbsp;

00:06:01.020 --> 00:06:08.760
So for somebody who has colored numbers,&nbsp;
normally we memorize the numbers or the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.760 --> 00:06:12.660
weekdays or the months or whatever thing we're&nbsp;
forced to arbitrarily memorize as a child&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.560 --> 00:06:20.880
for a synesthete, they tie it into something like&nbsp;
colors or in many cases, spatial location. So many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.880 --> 00:06:25.980
synesthetes have, let's say the months of the&nbsp;
year are laid out in front of them. So they say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.980 --> 00:06:30.420
oh, March is over here and April's kind of up&nbsp;
and to the right of it. And then May is down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.420 --> 00:06:34.680
here and June, July and it usually circles&nbsp;
around their body, that kind of thing. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.680 --> 00:06:40.487
they have a very spatial sense of the months or&nbsp;
the years or the number line, things like that.&nbsp;

00:06:40.487 --> 00:06:46.560
Tim Ferriss: So we may come back to that, but I&nbsp;
would like to jump to something that I think is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.340 --> 00:06:56.580
certainly adjacent and that is creating new senses&nbsp;
in some respects, and this makes me think of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.300 --> 00:07:02.400
one of your probably several TED talks, but can we&nbsp;
create new senses for humans? I saw this quite a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.400 --> 00:07:08.700
long time ago. I put it in my newsletter 5-Bullet&nbsp;
Friday, because I thought it was very much worth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.700 --> 00:07:13.800
watching and considering, not just considering&nbsp;
what you had then, but the implications of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.800 --> 00:07:19.620
developing technology. So would you mind&nbsp;
describing outside of congenital synesthesia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.620 --> 00:07:26.580
outside of gobbling psilocybe mushrooms and&nbsp;
seeing the Andromeda Galaxy appear before you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.580 --> 00:07:36.780
as you hear Pink Floyd, there are other options&nbsp;
for developing a new ability or new faculty to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.780 --> 00:07:42.686
perceive the world. So would you mind speaking to&nbsp;
sensory addition, if that's the right term to use?&nbsp;

00:07:42.686 --> 00:07:43.620
David Eagleman: Yeah.
Tim Ferriss: And maybe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.620 --> 00:07:46.500
we start with recent progress.
David Eagleman: The whole thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.500 --> 00:07:50.700
that got me interested in this a while ago was&nbsp;
understanding, just really thinking about the fact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.700 --> 00:07:56.460
that the brain is locked in silence and darkness&nbsp;
inside the skull. And all it ever sees are these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.460 --> 00:08:01.500
little electrical signals, which also can cause&nbsp;
chemical release and so on. But you've just got&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.500 --> 00:08:07.140
these electrochemical signals running around in&nbsp;
the dark, and yet when you open your eyes and look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.140 --> 00:08:10.980
at the world, you see everything in full color&nbsp;
and it looks so rich and so on, and you're hearing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.980 --> 00:08:17.640
music and you're feeling things on your fingertips&nbsp;
and you're smelling cinnamon. And all these things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.640 --> 00:08:23.460
seem like very distinct senses to you, but&nbsp;
they're all made of exactly the same stuff,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.300 --> 00:08:30.060
which is these electrochemical spikes. And so&nbsp;
I got really interested in this idea of could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.060 --> 00:08:35.880
we feed information into the brain via an unusual&nbsp;
source, and would the brain just figure it out?&nbsp;

00:08:35.880 --> 00:08:43.020
And so what we did is we built a vest that had&nbsp;
vibratory motors on it, and this was for people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.020 --> 00:08:48.120
who were deaf. It can capture sound and turn&nbsp;
the sound into patterns of vibration on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.120 --> 00:08:57.180
skin from high to low frequency, which is exactly&nbsp;
what the inner ear does. And we put it on deaf&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.180 --> 00:09:02.880
people and train them. And it turns out that works&nbsp;
great. You can learn to hear through your skin.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.660 --> 00:09:07.560
And it's because essentially the brain doesn't&nbsp;
care how the information gets there as long as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.560 --> 00:09:10.800
it gets there somehow, the brain will figure&nbsp;
out what to do with it. And the way it does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.800 --> 00:09:18.180
is by making correlations with other senses.&nbsp;
So you see the dog's mouth move and you feel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.180 --> 00:09:22.740
the barking on your skin and your brain figures&nbsp;
out, oh, that's the sound that goes with that.&nbsp;

00:09:23.520 --> 00:09:31.560
And so I gave that TED Talk on it where I showed&nbsp;
the vest, and then in the interim, what I did from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.560 --> 00:09:36.420
that time is I shrunk it down to a wristband.&nbsp;
I actually spun a company off, so I should say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.420 --> 00:09:40.440
we shrunk it down because the whole company was&nbsp;
involved in this very hard work, shrunk it down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.440 --> 00:09:46.080
to a wristband, and we're on wrists all over&nbsp;
the world. And that's not only for deafness,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.080 --> 00:09:50.820
but also for tinnitus, ringing in the ears. I&nbsp;
can explain how that works if interesting, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.820 --> 00:09:56.280
also for age-related hearing loss. So as people&nbsp;
get older, they lose their high frequencies.&nbsp;

00:09:56.280 --> 00:10:02.700
So we just use machine learning on this wristband&nbsp;
that listens in real time for the high frequency&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.700 --> 00:10:07.980
parts of speech like a TH or an S or a V or&nbsp;
a B, and it buzzes your wrist in different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.980 --> 00:10:13.440
ways to let you know, Hey, I just heard a TH,&nbsp;
I heard A, B, a V. And so what happens is your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.440 --> 00:10:18.660
ear is doing all the work at the medium and low&nbsp;
frequencies and the wristband is just clarifying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.660 --> 00:10:23.580
what just got said at the high frequency.&nbsp;
And turns out that works like gangbusters.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.580 --> 00:10:30.300
Your brain can learn to fuse those signals quite&nbsp;
easily, and after a few weeks people, they don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.300 --> 00:10:35.280
need hearing aids. It replaces a hearing aid.
Tim Ferriss: So your mind fills in the blanks once&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.280 --> 00:10:39.240
you develop the ability to associate properly.
David Eagleman: Exactly, because your mind is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.240 --> 00:10:42.300
getting the information now through your&nbsp;
wrist, through the skin of your wrist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.300 --> 00:10:47.460
which is essentially acting like a perfect inner&nbsp;
ear. We're just transferring the inner ear to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.460 --> 00:10:51.660
skin essentially. And that just passes the&nbsp;
information into the brain. The brain says,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.660 --> 00:10:57.900
"Oh, I got it. That was sheep versus sheet versus&nbsp;
sheik," just figures out, it gets the information&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.900 --> 00:11:02.280
from the wrist and puts it all together.
Tim Ferriss: So for the wrists to work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.280 --> 00:11:08.220
like the vest did, what was the most important&nbsp;
technological innovation? Because I think of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.220 --> 00:11:15.360
the surface area of the vest, I'm like, okay,&nbsp;
your ability to discern, say, vibration of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.360 --> 00:11:20.700
chest versus the back would be fairly, it would&nbsp;
appear to be fairly straightforward, but once you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.700 --> 00:11:28.500
constrain that to surface area and contact on the&nbsp;
wrist, I would imagine that probably incorrectly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.500 --> 00:11:34.184
but I would imagine that to be much more difficult&nbsp;
for the user to parse and learn to decipher.&nbsp;

00:11:34.184 --> 00:11:38.040
David Eagleman: It's actually not&nbsp;
because you have very low spatial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.040 --> 00:11:41.744
resolution on your torso. In other words —
Tim Ferriss: Right. It's the density.&nbsp;

00:11:41.744 --> 00:11:42.644
David Eagleman: Exactly.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.&nbsp;

00:11:42.644 --> 00:11:46.320
David Eagleman: So you have higher on the wrist,&nbsp;
but your intuition is right that there's just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.320 --> 00:11:52.800
less room on the wrist to stick motors. So we have&nbsp;
fewer motors than we had on the vest. It turns out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.520 --> 00:12:01.260
we use an illusion, which is if I stimulate&nbsp;
two neighboring motors, you will feel one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.260 --> 00:12:05.504
virtual point in between those two motors.
Tim Ferriss: It's like binaural beats with audio.&nbsp;

00:12:05.504 --> 00:12:09.420
David Eagleman: Yeah, exactly. And as I&nbsp;
change the amplitude of those two motors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.420 --> 00:12:14.580
I can move that virtual point around. So&nbsp;
we're actually stimulating 128 virtual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.580 --> 00:12:17.640
points on the wrist is what we're doing.
Tim Ferriss: Oh, that's clever. You clever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.640 --> 00:12:28.140
bastards. Good for you. So I wanted to&nbsp;
ask about maybe not replacement therapy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.140 --> 00:12:33.180
in the sense of hearing, say hearing&nbsp;
loss, fixing hearing loss, or fixing a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.320 --> 00:12:38.280
pathology, I think that would be fair to say&nbsp;
with tinnitus or tinnitus, which I happen to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.280 --> 00:12:42.720
have. So I'd be curious to know how that works.&nbsp;
It's not very pervasive in my lived experience,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:42.720 --> 00:12:49.260
but it does happen. I'd love to know also,&nbsp;
so this is a very sloppy compound question,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.260 --> 00:12:52.500
you can choose as you would at a&nbsp;
buffet where you'd like to go with it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.480 --> 00:13:00.240
What might exist for healthy normals in&nbsp;
terms of augmenting the scope of their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:00.240 --> 00:13:04.260
sensory experience? I don't expect it will all&nbsp;
become manta shrimp or something like that.&nbsp;

00:13:04.260 --> 00:13:08.580
But I'll mention a name that people might&nbsp;
want to look up. Ed Cooke, and I don't know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:08.580 --> 00:13:13.500
if you know this name, but he trained the author&nbsp;
behind a book called Moonwalking with Einstein&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.500 --> 00:13:18.540
to win the National Memory Championships&nbsp;
in the United States. Ed himself, very,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.540 --> 00:13:24.660
very high level memory competitor, also fascinated&nbsp;
with sensory substitution and sensory addition.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:25.500 --> 00:13:32.220
So maybe we could start with the tinnitus&nbsp;
or tinnitus and then go from there to what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:32.220 --> 00:13:36.688
do you think the applications are or could&nbsp;
be for healthy normals, if that makes sense.&nbsp;

00:13:36.688 --> 00:13:38.640
David Eagleman: Terrific. Okay.&nbsp;
So first thing by the way is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:39.240 --> 00:13:43.607
everyone calls it tinnitus and audiologists&nbsp;
call it tinnitus and either one is correct, so.&nbsp;

00:13:43.607 --> 00:13:45.240
Tim Ferriss: Okay, great.
David Eagleman: Second thing is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:45.240 --> 00:13:51.660
the way it works is something that is so simple,&nbsp;
it's called bimodal stimulation, but all it is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:51.660 --> 00:13:59.040
is we're playing tones, boo, boo, boo, boo, boo,&nbsp;
boo, around your tinnitus frequency, and you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:59.040 --> 00:14:04.140
feeling the corresponding buzzes on your skin. So&nbsp;
you're hearing the sound in your ears and you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:04.140 --> 00:14:09.780
feeling the buzz on your skin. That drives down&nbsp;
the tinnitus as much as any solution that exists.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:09.780 --> 00:14:14.400
The reason it works, there are very complicated&nbsp;
arguments, but I think the simple thing is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:14.400 --> 00:14:20.340
you're teaching the brain what is a real external&nbsp;
sound because the skin is giving you verification&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:20.340 --> 00:14:23.324
that the sound actually is real.
Tim Ferriss: It is external.&nbsp;

00:14:23.324 --> 00:14:27.467
David Eagleman: Yeah, exactly. As opposed to&nbsp;
the beep on the inside, which is fake news.&nbsp;

00:14:27.467 --> 00:14:31.106
Tim Ferriss: Or I guess originating externally.
David Eagleman: Yeah, exactly. Exactly.&nbsp;

00:14:31.106 --> 00:14:32.100
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.
David Eagleman: But the stuff on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:32.100 --> 00:14:36.840
inside isn't real and it doesn't get verification&nbsp;
or confirmation on the wrist. And so your brain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:36.840 --> 00:14:40.500
figures out, oh, okay, I don't need to pay as&nbsp;
much attention to that. So that's how that works,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:40.500 --> 00:14:42.900
which is cool. Now your question. Okay. So&nbsp;
that, great. So we're doing all these things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:42.900 --> 00:14:48.120
in the hearing space, and that's actually what my&nbsp;
company Neosensory is on the market with because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:48.120 --> 00:14:53.700
that's all a very direct path. But the really&nbsp;
interesting thing is we've got 70 projects,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:53.700 --> 00:15:00.660
seven zero different projects about sensory&nbsp;
addition and sensory expansion. And so some of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:00.660 --> 00:15:04.920
my favorites are things like for a long time I've&nbsp;
been wearing infrared light just so I can pick up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:04.920 --> 00:15:08.820
on what's going on in the infrared range. And you&nbsp;
can actually do this in the near infrared or the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:08.820 --> 00:15:12.660
mid-wave infrared. But in the near infrared, the&nbsp;
first time I put it on, I happened to be walking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:12.660 --> 00:15:17.040
at nighttime in between a couple houses and I&nbsp;
suddenly felt all this infrared radiation, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:17.040 --> 00:15:21.420
I couldn't figure out where it was coming from.
So I just followed my wrist and there was a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:21.420 --> 00:15:26.880
security camera with infrared LEDs, which normally&nbsp;
is totally invisible to you, you can't see that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:26.880 --> 00:15:31.860
But it was so obvious to me that the security&nbsp;
camera was there because I could feel it. But in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:31.860 --> 00:15:36.540
the mid-range, that's when it's really interesting&nbsp;
because that's the temperature, that's where it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:36.540 --> 00:15:42.780
picking up heat signatures. And so I can walk&nbsp;
through the parking lot and know immediately which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:42.780 --> 00:15:46.320
cars have been parked there a while and which ones&nbsp;
have just pulled up in the last half hour because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:46.320 --> 00:15:51.060
there's a lot of heat coming from the engine&nbsp;
block. I can just feel that. Or when I happened&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:51.060 --> 00:15:54.960
to go in the library the first time I was wearing&nbsp;
this, and I could tell there were two empty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:54.960 --> 00:15:59.340
chairs, I could tell which one was sat in at some&nbsp;
point in the last half hour because there's still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:59.340 --> 00:16:04.200
a heat signature there. And even the books on the&nbsp;
display where people have recently picked up a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:04.200 --> 00:16:08.940
book, they still leave the heat signature there.
So it's really amazing. It's like slicing up time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:08.940 --> 00:16:14.340
in a different way when you're tapped into the&nbsp;
infrared range. But we have all kinds of projects&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:14.340 --> 00:16:21.060
where you can feed in data from the internet,&nbsp;
stock market data, Twitter data. One of the cool&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:21.060 --> 00:16:26.460
things that we did is we took one of these smart&nbsp;
watches that can pick up on your gathering skin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:26.460 --> 00:16:31.440
response and heart rate and heart rate variability&nbsp;
and that stuff. And then we, using the API,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:31.440 --> 00:16:38.040
we fed that data out and over the internet and&nbsp;
then to the wristband. So you can feel all that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:38.040 --> 00:16:42.360
But the interesting part is when you take the&nbsp;
wristband off and put it on someone else. So let's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:42.360 --> 00:16:47.627
say your spouse is tapped into your physiology&nbsp;
and your spouse can feel the things that you're —&nbsp;

00:16:47.627 --> 00:16:49.560
Tim Ferriss: Time to leave&nbsp;
Tim alone. He's dysregulated.&nbsp;

00:16:49.560 --> 00:16:54.780
David Eagleman: Exactly. I have&nbsp;
really no idea if this is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:54.780 --> 00:16:59.880
useful thing for marriages, but it is totally&nbsp;
cool to be able to know when someone else is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:59.880 --> 00:17:02.640
feeling stressed out. I mean, you can be, the&nbsp;
reason we did this over the internet is you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:02.640 --> 00:17:06.600
can be on the other side of the globe and you&nbsp;
can suddenly call your wife and say, "Hey baby,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:06.600 --> 00:17:10.200
you stressed out? You're feeling okay?" And&nbsp;
anyway, it's just a really interesting thing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:10.200 --> 00:17:14.400
So we're exploring lots of things. None of&nbsp;
them have clear market paths, but they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:14.400 --> 00:17:19.680
the thing that feeds me scientifically.
Tim Ferriss: Of the 70 experiments/projects&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:20.940 --> 00:17:25.000
that you have running, why did you&nbsp;
choose the infrared for yourself?&nbsp;

00:17:25.000 --> 00:17:28.920
David Eagleman: Oh, because several of&nbsp;
them are clinical. They have to do it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:28.920 --> 00:17:33.420
let's say for example, for prosthetic legs&nbsp;
or peripheral neuropathies or whatever,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:33.420 --> 00:17:37.800
we're just feeding data in. But because&nbsp;
I don't have any of those problems,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:38.340 --> 00:17:43.860
not yet, knock on wood, it turns out that I'm&nbsp;
into the idea of sensory expansion. Can I tap&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:43.860 --> 00:17:48.180
into other things? And one of the things as maybe&nbsp;
you remember from my TED Talk, one of the things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:48.180 --> 00:17:55.680
I've always been fascinated by is how little we&nbsp;
see of the world. We see such a tiny fraction of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:55.680 --> 00:18:01.320
what is happening out there. So I've always wanted&nbsp;
to be tapped into a lot more. Just as an example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:01.320 --> 00:18:06.720
you may remember this from the TED talk, the&nbsp;
amount what we call visible light, the ROYGBIV,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:06.720 --> 00:18:11.940
that's less than a 10 trillionth of the light&nbsp;
that's out there of the electromagnetic spectrum.&nbsp;

00:18:11.940 --> 00:18:18.840
And so it turns out that all these other things,&nbsp;
x-rays, microwaves, and gamma rays and all these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:18.840 --> 00:18:22.860
things are passing through your body totally&nbsp;
invisible to you because you don't have the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:22.860 --> 00:18:29.700
proper receptors to pick up on it. But I suspect&nbsp;
there are a whole bunch of Nobel Prizes hidden&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:29.700 --> 00:18:34.380
out there in different parts of the spectrum if&nbsp;
we could just pick up on those and detect them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:34.920 --> 00:18:40.260
in our daily lives and be aware of things. And&nbsp;
by the way, I should clarify one thing on that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:40.260 --> 00:18:45.480
We have all kinds of ways of detecting things&nbsp;
in the spectrum in those different parts, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:46.080 --> 00:18:50.580
only in the laboratory. We're not walking&nbsp;
around and experiencing the world that way.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:50.580 --> 00:18:55.140
And I think that's what makes all the difference.
I'll just give you one example, just to clarify&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:55.140 --> 00:19:01.740
what I'm saying. Some colleagues of mine have this&nbsp;
company where they look in the microwave range at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:01.740 --> 00:19:05.880
the oceans to see where the ships are going, and&nbsp;
they can make estimates of commerce that way and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:05.880 --> 00:19:10.440
so on. But what they discovered quite accidentally&nbsp;
is that if you look at water in the microwave&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:10.440 --> 00:19:14.100
range, you can tell if it's drinkable or not.&nbsp;
You can tell what water's drinkable and what's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:14.100 --> 00:19:17.580
not. And nobody knew that before. It's just one&nbsp;
of these things where you have to look and say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:17.580 --> 00:19:24.360
"Oh, hey, did you notice this?" And so that's what&nbsp;
I think we are set up for is so many discoveries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:24.360 --> 00:19:29.340
of just being in the world in different,&nbsp;
having different types of vision on the world.&nbsp;

00:19:29.340 --> 00:19:35.580
Tim Ferriss: If you had to maybe edge&nbsp;
into the realm of science fiction, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:35.580 --> 00:19:41.820
informed science fiction, could just be informed&nbsp;
projection, let's say in the next five years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:42.960 --> 00:19:49.560
what would be your wildest hopes or dreams&nbsp;
for the future of this type of technology?&nbsp;

00:19:49.560 --> 00:19:54.060
David Eagleman: One of them is that I think we&nbsp;
can really use AI and machine learning for a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:54.060 --> 00:19:59.220
of very useful stuff. I'll give you one example&nbsp;
of actually a company that's spun off from our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:59.220 --> 00:20:05.640
company, which is it's these three college&nbsp;
students who are doing this awesome thing to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:06.240 --> 00:20:12.240
help people with autism understand the social&nbsp;
context that they're in. So kids with autism&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:12.240 --> 00:20:17.160
usually can't tell if the person they're talking&nbsp;
to is angry or happy or sad or whatever. So the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:17.160 --> 00:20:23.760
wristband just uses machine learning to listen&nbsp;
and say, oh, that person is angry or happy or sad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:23.760 --> 00:20:28.320
or whatever, and then just buzzes to tell you the&nbsp;
right answer. And this kind of thing is so useful.&nbsp;

00:20:29.760 --> 00:20:34.980
Obviously we are in the middle of this explosion&nbsp;
of what's going on in AI. And to be able to use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:34.980 --> 00:20:39.720
that and do some very sophisticated thing and&nbsp;
then just feed the data back into the user and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:39.720 --> 00:20:43.860
just tell the user the simple answer to the very&nbsp;
complicated thing going on out there, I think,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:43.860 --> 00:20:48.540
we're just going to see so much of that happening.
Tim Ferriss: Are there any other applications&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:49.080 --> 00:20:54.120
that come to mind? And it doesn't have to&nbsp;
be human centric. It could be something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:55.680 --> 00:21:01.680
like the augmentation you described with&nbsp;
microwave range and the potability of water.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:02.520 --> 00:21:07.560
But do any other applications come to mind?
David Eagleman: One that we proposed a long&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:07.560 --> 00:21:11.700
time ago and we sort of did a tiny bit&nbsp;
of work on is about stock market data,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:11.700 --> 00:21:15.960
summarizing what's happening in the stock market&nbsp;
and moment to moment, you can feel it on your skin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:17.280 --> 00:21:21.240
or there are many ways to do it, with whole&nbsp;
patterns or just like, hey, I want to follow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:21.240 --> 00:21:27.420
Google and Meta and whatever. And you can feel&nbsp;
what's going on. And what I'm interested in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:28.380 --> 00:21:32.160
is if we train someone for the long term on&nbsp;
this, can they get to the point where they say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:32.820 --> 00:21:37.140
"Hey, I just have this feeling, this gut feeling&nbsp;
that oil is crashing right now," or something,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:37.140 --> 00:21:42.120
without even being perceptually aware of what all&nbsp;
these signals are and what they mean. Can they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:42.120 --> 00:21:45.947
just develop a direct perceptual experience&nbsp;
of the economic movements of the planet.&nbsp;

00:21:45.947 --> 00:21:50.267
Tim Ferriss: Just developing trading intuitives.
David Eagleman: Yeah. Yeah.&nbsp;

00:21:50.267 --> 00:21:53.024
Tim Ferriss: By feeding&nbsp;
them lots and lots of data.&nbsp;

00:21:53.024 --> 00:21:57.420
David Eagleman: Because that's all&nbsp;
vision and hearing and all this stuff is.&nbsp;

00:21:57.420 --> 00:21:58.380
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.
David Eagleman: We experience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:58.380 --> 00:22:01.620
lots of data. When you're a kid, when you're a&nbsp;
baby, you don't know how the heck to use your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:01.620 --> 00:22:05.700
eyes or ears or whatever. You're just getting all&nbsp;
this data going into the darkness there and you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:05.700 --> 00:22:09.420
figure stuff out. You figure out correlations.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah. Well, it also ties in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:09.420 --> 00:22:14.880
and I suspect we won't go too deeply on this, but&nbsp;
relates to AI and also just technology in general,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:14.880 --> 00:22:17.880
machine learning as well, because&nbsp;
it turns out some of the things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:17.880 --> 00:22:22.260
we might have assumed to be very, very&nbsp;
complex like playing chess, playing go,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:23.340 --> 00:22:26.207
are very easy to train machines for.
David Eagleman: Yes.&nbsp;

00:22:26.207 --> 00:22:31.920
Tim Ferriss: Whereas if you want a machine&nbsp;
to catch a ball, if you want a machine to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:31.920 --> 00:22:39.000
walk in some capacity, do things that we take&nbsp;
for granted, drive a car, some of the simplest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:39.000 --> 00:22:46.500
things turn out to be the hardest things. And it&nbsp;
seems to be that underscores in a way how unaware&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:46.500 --> 00:22:53.198
most of us are of how much we are processing&nbsp;
at any given point in time. It's remarkable.&nbsp;

00:22:53.198 --> 00:22:57.300
David Eagleman: Yeah. This, by the way, is why I&nbsp;
wrote my book Incognito some years ago, which is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:57.300 --> 00:23:01.320
about everything that's running under the hood&nbsp;
that we don't have access to. I mean, the amount&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:02.100 --> 00:23:08.640
that your conscious mind has access to, it's&nbsp;
the broom closet in the mansion of the brain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:09.720 --> 00:23:12.840
the part where you say, oh yeah,&nbsp;
I'm aware of this. I mean, look,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:12.840 --> 00:23:17.700
I'm raising this cup of water to my mouth. I have&nbsp;
no idea how I'm doing the thing with my arm and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:17.700 --> 00:23:21.900
my elbow and I'm getting into my mouth. All I am&nbsp;
aware of is whether I spilled it on myself or not.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:21.900 --> 00:23:25.920
And it's like that with everything in your life,&nbsp;
getting a joke, falling in love, driving a car,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:25.920 --> 00:23:31.380
all this stuff is happening totally unconsciously.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah. How do you think these days,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:32.220 --> 00:23:36.780
this is going to be a bit of a broad question,&nbsp;
there's probably a better way to put it, but about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:36.780 --> 00:23:44.160
memory and specifically the reason behind the&nbsp;
question or recall — let me make it more specific&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:44.160 --> 00:23:54.000
— is that we learn, we forget or we use these&nbsp;
terms in ways that imply we know what those mean.&nbsp;

00:23:54.660 --> 00:24:02.460
And I've had experiences where, say, I studied&nbsp;
language long ago, 10, 15, 20 years ago. At the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:02.460 --> 00:24:07.680
time I was pretty decent, but if you were to put&nbsp;
me in circumstances that require German right now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:07.680 --> 00:24:17.400
I would not be able to perform. But in certain&nbsp;
states, whether that's in, say, a non-ordinary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:17.400 --> 00:24:22.800
state of consciousness induced by psychedelic&nbsp;
compounds or others, it's not the only one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:24.480 --> 00:24:31.380
in periods of say, very deep ketosis where&nbsp;
I'm doing, say, an extended fast and I have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:31.380 --> 00:24:39.600
2.53 millimolars of BHB measured by finger prick.&nbsp;
I do then appear to have access to these memories&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:39.600 --> 00:24:44.760
I had assumed I had "forgotten" in quotation&nbsp;
marks. How do you think about these things?&nbsp;

00:24:44.760 --> 00:24:50.760
David Eagleman: First of all, I'd love to do&nbsp;
actual test with you on the, like what I mean is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:50.760 --> 00:24:55.500
for example, we could just pull up online&nbsp;
some basic German test where you're doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:55.500 --> 00:24:59.722
something and see whether, when in deep&nbsp;
ketosis, if you have a better score —&nbsp;

00:24:59.722 --> 00:25:00.404
Tim Ferriss: Sure.
David Eagleman: — than you do otherwise.&nbsp;

00:25:00.404 --> 00:25:02.820
Tim Ferriss: To verify it. For sure.
David Eagleman: Yeah. Because if that is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:02.820 --> 00:25:07.680
verifiable, that's amazing and cool. And what&nbsp;
it illustrates is something that we do know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:07.680 --> 00:25:13.560
which is that that stuff is in there. And we&nbsp;
know this for several reasons. I mean, big one is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:14.520 --> 00:25:18.900
if somebody says a German word, let's say that&nbsp;
you haven't heard in years, but you say, "Oh yeah,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:18.900 --> 00:25:22.560
that's right. I remember that word." I mean, all&nbsp;
that stuff's in there. The other thing is there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:22.560 --> 00:25:27.840
a psychological effect called savings, which is,&nbsp;
let's say you've studied German for a long time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:27.840 --> 00:25:31.710
then you don't study it for years, but then you&nbsp;
pick it up again and you say, okay, I'm going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:31.710 --> 00:25:36.240
get really serious and study this. You'll be much&nbsp;
faster at learning it because all that data's in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:36.240 --> 00:25:40.920
there, or in some form, it's in there. And so&nbsp;
it's much, much faster the second time around.&nbsp;

00:25:42.480 --> 00:25:48.240
Just as an example, when astronauts come back&nbsp;
from orbit, they have to relearn how to walk,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:48.240 --> 00:25:52.440
but it doesn't take them as long as it does an&nbsp;
infant. They're first, they can't really walk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:52.440 --> 00:25:57.540
on their own, and then they're walking fine. So&nbsp;
yeah. What is fascinating, and maybe this is part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:57.540 --> 00:26:02.880
of what you were getting at with your question,&nbsp;
is we use the word memory in computers, and that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:02.880 --> 00:26:07.680
such a shame that we use the same word because&nbsp;
there's hardly any relationship there. Memory in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:07.680 --> 00:26:10.800
a computer is perfect. You're putting in zeros and&nbsp;
ones. You get those same zeros and ones back out.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:11.580 --> 00:26:21.240
In a human brain, it's a totally different thing&nbsp;
going on. And in my last book, Livewired, I talk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:21.240 --> 00:26:25.560
about how memory actually has to get moved around&nbsp;
in the brain and moved into different states,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:26.580 --> 00:26:30.480
and there's all kinds of weird stuff.
Here I'll just, I'll flag one or two, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:30.480 --> 00:26:37.200
is one of them is that memory actually has to be&nbsp;
reconsolidated after you take it out of long-term&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:37.200 --> 00:26:41.040
storage. So maybe you knew this, but so let's say&nbsp;
to a listener, if I ask you, "What's the name of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:41.040 --> 00:26:45.960
your fifth grade teacher?" You haven't thought&nbsp;
about that presumably in a very long time. So you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:45.960 --> 00:26:51.240
bring that out of cold storage, let's call it, and&nbsp;
you're thinking about it now. Now it's an active&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:51.240 --> 00:26:55.920
memory represented by spikes running around in&nbsp;
the brain. If you get hit in the head right now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:56.880 --> 00:27:01.367
that'll be gone. It'll be gone. Or if I&nbsp;
give you a protein synthesis inhibitor —&nbsp;

00:27:01.367 --> 00:27:06.120
Tim Ferriss: Meaning that you've brought&nbsp;
it to the bullpen, so to speak, but now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:06.120 --> 00:27:09.660
it's vulnerable to interruption in such a way&nbsp;
that it would not make its way back to cold&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:09.660 --> 00:27:11.520
storage. Is that what you mean?
David Eagleman: That's exactly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:11.520 --> 00:27:12.559
right. That's exactly right.
Tim Ferriss: Wow.&nbsp;

00:27:12.559 --> 00:27:13.484
David Eagleman: Because the memory needs to be —
Tim Ferriss: Yeah. Did not realize that.&nbsp;

00:27:13.484 --> 00:27:17.520
David Eagleman: Yeah, a memory needs to be&nbsp;
reconsolidated. It needs to be taken from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:17.520 --> 00:27:25.140
activity back into the structure of the system.&nbsp;
And so it's vulnerable to erasure. Super weird,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:25.140 --> 00:27:32.520
but I'll just as a side note, scary memories, very&nbsp;
emotionally charged memories, are not erasable.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:32.520 --> 00:27:37.440
Those are unerasable, which sucks actually,&nbsp;
because those are the ones that trauma victims&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:37.440 --> 00:27:44.340
often want to erase. But you have what amounts to&nbsp;
a secondary memory system that says, "Hold on to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:44.340 --> 00:27:47.760
that, because that's the really important stuff."
Tim Ferriss: Are there any researchers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:48.840 --> 00:27:58.140
who come to mind, could be thinkers, writers,&nbsp;
who are doing a really interesting job of just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:58.140 --> 00:28:03.780
examining the question of what, if anything,&nbsp;
gets permanently deleted to come back to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:03.780 --> 00:28:09.720
human versus computer? Because I've been&nbsp;
shocked how things can surface from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:09.720 --> 00:28:16.440
recesses of my mind that I assumed had long been&nbsp;
sent to the landfill. There's let's just say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:16.440 --> 00:28:21.180
this active working memory, then you have the&nbsp;
cold storage, long term. And I assumed okay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:21.180 --> 00:28:25.140
at some point, the warehouse is full. Things&nbsp;
get taken to the landfill. But lo and behold,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:26.100 --> 00:28:33.240
certainly recognition is easier, but at points,&nbsp;
I've also had the recall. Is there any particular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:33.240 --> 00:28:40.920
researcher or any resources or institutes&nbsp;
who are looking at this in interesting ways?&nbsp;

00:28:40.920 --> 00:28:43.320
David Eagleman: That's an interesting&nbsp;
question. That's a very tough question&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:43.320 --> 00:28:47.580
to address. It's something that we&nbsp;
make observations on in our own lives.&nbsp;

00:28:47.580 --> 00:28:50.940
Tim Ferriss: How would you test it?
David Eagleman: That's a particularly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:50.940 --> 00:28:54.060
challenging one. But people are doing all&nbsp;
kinds — one of the things that people do test,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:54.060 --> 00:28:59.160
which is slightly different than what you're&nbsp;
asking, but it's about the way that memory —&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:59.160 --> 00:29:04.740
even though we believe the part that we do&nbsp;
remember is accurate, it's the way that drifts and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:06.060 --> 00:29:09.600
the way that memory is not so accurate.
Just as an example, my colleague Liz Phelps,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:09.600 --> 00:29:16.860
at NYU, right after September 11, 2001, she went&nbsp;
and interviewed a bunch of people and said, "What&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:16.860 --> 00:29:22.620
exactly did you see on September 11th?" And she&nbsp;
was also clever enough to ask them, "Tell me your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:22.620 --> 00:29:26.580
memories from September 10th," the day before.&nbsp;
And they said, "Okay, well I ate lunch here. I did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:26.580 --> 00:29:30.840
this," whatever. Okay. Then she found these same&nbsp;
people a year later and asked them, and it turns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:30.840 --> 00:29:36.000
out both the highly emotional charged memories and&nbsp;
the boring memories had both drifted. It turns out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:36.000 --> 00:29:41.160
that everything drifts. Even things that you think&nbsp;
you could never get wrong, people get wrong all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:41.160 --> 00:29:46.920
the time. So memory is a myth-making machine, and&nbsp;
we're constantly reinventing our past, especially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:46.920 --> 00:29:51.000
as we tell the stories over and over again.
Tim Ferriss: Totally. This is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:51.600 --> 00:30:01.020
part of why I'm very skeptical of certain, say,&nbsp;
studies involving nutrition that are observational&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:01.020 --> 00:30:06.840
studies where they rely on self-reporting, but&nbsp;
people are filling in the diary of what they ate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:06.840 --> 00:30:13.500
asynchronously. And I'm like, oh, man. I would bet&nbsp;
people are going to be 20 percent off minimum on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:13.500 --> 00:30:17.100
average with that type of reconstruction.
David Eagleman: That doesn't even include&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:17.100 --> 00:30:20.100
the purposeful lying that people do on that.
Tim Ferriss: Right. Right. Right, exactly. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:20.100 --> 00:30:24.720
let's shift maybe back in time a bit. I know&nbsp;
we've been talking about a number of the books,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:24.720 --> 00:30:32.340
and I'm going to come back to Livewired,&nbsp;
but I wanted to ask you about influences.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:32.340 --> 00:30:37.080
And maybe we could start with one&nbsp;
who some folks will recognize. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:37.800 --> 00:30:43.500
Francis Crick, how did you meet Francis Crick?
David Eagleman: I did my post-doctoral fellowship&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:43.500 --> 00:30:47.940
at a place called the Salk Institute,&nbsp;
and that's where Francis Crick was. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:48.960 --> 00:30:52.740
for anyone who doesn't know, he was the&nbsp;
co-discoverer of the structure of DNA&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:53.460 --> 00:30:58.800
and just figured out all these massively&nbsp;
important things in molecular biology.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:58.800 --> 00:31:05.220
He was essentially the titan of the 20th&nbsp;
century in biology. And I, like many people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:05.220 --> 00:31:09.360
I think had assumed he had already passed&nbsp;
away or something. I didn't realize he was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:09.360 --> 00:31:14.280
still trucking along. But the thing that was so&nbsp;
special about that guy was that he didn't have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:14.280 --> 00:31:21.000
any of the constraints that other scientists did.
So other scientists all have to whatever. We write&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:21.000 --> 00:31:25.080
grants. We write papers. We have to do mentoring&nbsp;
grad students, whatever, reading applications.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:25.740 --> 00:31:29.940
But because he won his Nobel Prize when he was&nbsp;
young, he just didn't have any of that. And as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:29.940 --> 00:31:36.000
a result, he was able to just spend a 100 percent&nbsp;
of his time doing deep thinking, and that was it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:36.000 --> 00:31:40.200
I walked into his office, he had a stack of&nbsp;
all the journals there, Science, and Nature,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:40.200 --> 00:31:42.900
and Journal of Neuroscience, and so on. And he&nbsp;
was flipping through it and one day I asked him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:42.900 --> 00:31:45.780
I said, "What are you looking for?" Because it&nbsp;
looked like he was looking for something. He said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:45.780 --> 00:31:51.300
"I don't know. I'm just looking." And so anyway,&nbsp;
he was an amazing guy, precisely because he was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:51.300 --> 00:31:55.920
so high-IQ, so high-octane, and had no other&nbsp;
constraints on him. I've never known anyone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:55.920 --> 00:32:00.540
who happened to be in that position before.
Tim Ferriss: So when you mentioned the 70&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:00.540 --> 00:32:06.000
projects, which I understand is within the context&nbsp;
of a for-profit company, so it would make sense to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:06.000 --> 00:32:11.280
examine a lot of things. But if I just look&nbsp;
at your bio, I see many different projects,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:11.280 --> 00:32:19.140
many different irons in the fire, and I'm looking&nbsp;
at an older New Yorker piece. This is from 2011,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:19.140 --> 00:32:26.640
and as it relates to Crick who died in 2004. So&nbsp;
the advice, and you can't believe everything you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:26.640 --> 00:32:31.380
read, but I'd love to hear you maybe expand&nbsp;
on this or speak to how it impacted you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:32.460 --> 00:32:38.520
is, "The dangerous man," this is quoting Crick,&nbsp;
"The dangerous man is one who has only one idea&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:38.520 --> 00:32:43.260
because then he'll fight and die for it. The way&nbsp;
real science goes is that you come up with lots of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:43.260 --> 00:32:46.140
ideas and most of them will be wrong."
David Eagleman: Right.&nbsp;

00:32:46.140 --> 00:32:51.420
Tim Ferriss: What impact did that have on you?
David Eagleman: Well, I think, if anything,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:51.420 --> 00:32:55.423
that just meshed with the way&nbsp;
I already thought about things.&nbsp;

00:32:55.423 --> 00:33:00.120
Tim Ferriss: You confirmed what you wanted to do.
David Eagleman: Exactly. Hold on, let me take a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:00.120 --> 00:33:04.260
one-second tangent here, which is the worst piece&nbsp;
of advice I ever had was when I was in college,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:04.260 --> 00:33:07.800
there was a professor, an electrical engineering&nbsp;
professor of mine, and I really liked the guy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:07.800 --> 00:33:12.600
And he said to me one day, he said, "Look,&nbsp;
Eagleman, life is like you're a lumberjack,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:12.600 --> 00:33:17.040
and you go into the forest with your ax. You've&nbsp;
got to pick one tree, and you just hit that tree.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:17.040 --> 00:33:21.000
If you take a whack at all the trees, you're not&nbsp;
going to get anywhere." And it sounded so wise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:21.000 --> 00:33:26.940
and so reasonable and so on, but it's just not&nbsp;
who I am. I'm just not the pick-one-tree guy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:26.940 --> 00:33:32.880
and so I think it already meshed with —
Tim Ferriss: You're preaching to the choir here.

00:33:32.880 --> 00:33:38.160
David Eagleman: Yeah, exactly. So when Crick said&nbsp;
that — and by the way, any good scientist is doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:38.160 --> 00:33:42.960
this anyway. You have lots of hypotheses, each one&nbsp;
of which you think, "Wow. That's going to crack&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:42.960 --> 00:33:46.740
open something big," and then you realize, "Oh,&nbsp;
that's totally wrong." Or you run an experiment or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:46.740 --> 00:33:48.867
whatever. So you got to have a lot of them.
MIDROLL

00:33:48.867 --> 00:33:53.040
Tim Ferriss: So let's say&nbsp;
you didn't have the podcast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:53.040 --> 00:33:56.760
which people should check out,&nbsp;
Inner Cosmos. David Eagleman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:56.760 --> 00:34:03.360
didn't have the production company. You didn't&nbsp;
have any obligations outside of science, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:03.900 --> 00:34:09.300
you had done something, maybe it was winning a&nbsp;
Nobel Prize, where you had the same ability to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:09.300 --> 00:34:17.460
sit in your office, sit in your lab, and basically&nbsp;
do whatever you want. What types of, outside of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:17.460 --> 00:34:23.820
reading widely and having piles of things on your&nbsp;
desk perhaps Crick did, are there any hypotheses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:24.660 --> 00:34:31.560
or experiments that you would have at the top&nbsp;
of your list of things to pay attention to?&nbsp;

00:34:31.560 --> 00:34:37.500
David Eagleman: Most of my career has been&nbsp;
defined by trying to understand how the brain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:37.500 --> 00:34:40.980
constructs reality. You've got all these spikes&nbsp;
running around, and the question is, how does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:40.980 --> 00:34:46.620
that make this subjective reality we have with&nbsp;
all its colors and sounds and tastes and smells&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:46.620 --> 00:34:52.440
and so on? Why does this biological activity&nbsp;
in a whole bunch of cells feel like something?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:53.280 --> 00:34:58.860
That feels to me like the biggest central question&nbsp;
in neuroscience. But there are lots of — I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:58.860 --> 00:35:03.900
actually wrote an article, I think in 2004, 2006,&nbsp;
I wrote the cover article for Discover magazine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:03.900 --> 00:35:08.940
and I called it "10 Unsolved Questions of&nbsp;
[the Brain]." And I define 10 questions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:10.380 --> 00:35:16.020
and these are still equally as unsolved now&nbsp;
as they were way back then. And so I feel like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:17.580 --> 00:35:21.780
there's so much really rich stuff to answer.
But anyway, consciousness really sits at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:21.780 --> 00:35:24.900
middle. And by the way, for Francis Crick,&nbsp;
this was one of his central questions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:25.560 --> 00:35:30.480
maybe the central question that he devoted the end&nbsp;
of his life to. How do you put together 86 billion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:30.480 --> 00:35:36.300
cells and get experience, get meaning out of it?
Tim Ferriss: So let's talk about consciousness&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:36.300 --> 00:35:40.860
for a second, which is such a slippery pig. I know&nbsp;
some folks hate talking about it because they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:40.860 --> 00:35:44.160
like, "Ah, it's like talking about God. We might&nbsp;
not even be talking about the same thing. Let's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:44.160 --> 00:35:51.180
define terms." But for the moment, we can put that&nbsp;
aside. Although, if you have a tidy definition,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:51.180 --> 00:35:58.440
I'd love to hear it. But do you think that&nbsp;
consciousness is an emergent property? So at some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:58.440 --> 00:36:04.440
point these building blocks, these carbon-based&nbsp;
structures build up, and then we have at some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:04.440 --> 00:36:09.180
point consciousness as an emergent property, or&nbsp;
do you feel, and maybe there's a third answer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:09.180 --> 00:36:13.380
that it is more fundamental than that?
David Eagleman: I don't think there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:13.380 --> 00:36:18.420
any way to know. So just to define that for the&nbsp;
audience, so the second view is sometimes called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:18.420 --> 00:36:26.580
panpsychism, which is that maybe consciousness is&nbsp;
a fundamental quality of the cosmos and gravity or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:26.580 --> 00:36:33.120
mass or whatever. It's already there and you're&nbsp;
putting things together in just the right way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:33.840 --> 00:36:39.180
and the first version is that if you put a bunch&nbsp;
of cells together in just the right structure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:40.020 --> 00:36:47.520
you get self-awareness and sentience. We&nbsp;
don't really know, and all the data we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:47.520 --> 00:36:50.820
have is consistent with either. So the data&nbsp;
we have on consciousness, of course, is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:50.820 --> 00:36:56.880
if you hurt your brain, let's say with a stroke,&nbsp;
or tumor, or traumatic brain injury, or whatever,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:57.540 --> 00:37:05.340
or you put molecules in drugs, your consciousness&nbsp;
can change in any of these circumstances. But all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:05.340 --> 00:37:10.200
that tells us is that the physical integrity&nbsp;
of the system is necessary for the conscious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:10.200 --> 00:37:18.120
experience. But it certainly could be either.
Tim Ferriss: What was Crick trying to solve for,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:18.120 --> 00:37:26.640
or how was he trying to examine consciousness?&nbsp;
And I know panpsychism has developed a bit of a,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:26.640 --> 00:37:31.620
I don't want to say negative connotation, but&nbsp;
even some physicists who I guess are taking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:31.620 --> 00:37:37.860
the understanding that I couldn't even begin to&nbsp;
explain how one might test this experimentally.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:37.860 --> 00:37:43.440
So I assume it's largely theoretical at this&nbsp;
point, but those who take the consciousness&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:43.440 --> 00:37:52.860
as fundamental side of things seem to try not to&nbsp;
use panpsychism, some of them. But how was Crick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:52.860 --> 00:37:58.500
approaching this, and why was it so compelling?
David Eagleman: Well, let me say two things. Okay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:58.500 --> 00:38:03.000
so Crick had a chalkboard in his office,&nbsp;
and he had lots of things written on it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:03.000 --> 00:38:07.320
but right in the middle, he had the word&nbsp;
meaning written on it because that was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:07.320 --> 00:38:12.360
his central question. Why does any of this mean&nbsp;
anything to us? I mean, if I look at my garage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:12.360 --> 00:38:16.260
door opener or my cell phone or whatever, it's&nbsp;
just pushing zeros and ones around in transistors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:16.260 --> 00:38:22.380
and presumably nothing means anything to it,&nbsp;
but we have all this feeling and meaning. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:22.380 --> 00:38:27.900
that was his central question, and what he was&nbsp;
trying to figure out was what he called the NCC,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:27.900 --> 00:38:32.820
the neural correlates of consciousness.
So working with our mutual colleague,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:32.820 --> 00:38:38.580
Christof Koch, he was working on the NCC and&nbsp;
trying to figure out okay, look, there's the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:38.580 --> 00:38:42.300
hard problem of consciousness, which is why&nbsp;
does any of this equal subjective experience,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:42.300 --> 00:38:48.120
but the maybe easier problem is to figure out what&nbsp;
parts of the brain are involved and absolutely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:48.120 --> 00:38:54.240
have to be there for you to be consciously aware&nbsp;
of something. And so that's what he defined that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:54.240 --> 00:38:58.440
as a program and worked on that until he passed&nbsp;
away. But there are many other people who are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:58.440 --> 00:39:03.840
pursuing that in various ways. But again,&nbsp;
that's the easy problem of consciousness,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:03.840 --> 00:39:06.900
just figuring out the correlates.
Tim Ferriss: It's exciting.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:08.160 --> 00:39:15.000
Are there any of the 10 unsolved questions, if&nbsp;
I'm getting the headline right, of neuroscience,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:15.000 --> 00:39:21.660
of those 10 are if you're like, "You know what?&nbsp;
If I could have an XPRIZE, put something together&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:21.660 --> 00:39:27.960
to make it so financially compelling that some&nbsp;
of the best scientists in the world would drop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:27.960 --> 00:39:33.000
what they're doing to focus on this, this&nbsp;
is the one or the two that I might choose."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:34.020 --> 00:39:37.800
Are there any that come to mind? They don't&nbsp;
even necessarily need to be from that piece.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:37.800 --> 00:39:42.300
They could be something that simply comes to mind.
David Eagleman: Actually, I'll tell — let me give&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:42.300 --> 00:39:46.680
a slightly different answer to the question&nbsp;
because actually what I thought you might be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:46.680 --> 00:39:51.480
about to ask, "Are there any of the 10 that&nbsp;
we have made some progress on?" And actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:52.500 --> 00:39:57.780
there is one and that has to do with why&nbsp;
we dream. And one of the questions was why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:57.780 --> 00:40:01.980
do brains sleep and dream? Sleep is still a&nbsp;
little complicated, but they've a bunch of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:01.980 --> 00:40:06.780
hypothesis. But I think that my student&nbsp;
and I've solved why we dream at night,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:07.860 --> 00:40:12.060
and I can tell you if you're interested in that.
Tim Ferriss: No, no, thanks. We're in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:12.060 --> 00:40:15.960
rush here. Yeah, please go ahead, David.
David Eagleman: Which is, it has to do with brain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:15.960 --> 00:40:22.620
plasticity. So the brain is always reconfiguring&nbsp;
itself. It's a very dynamic system, and every one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:22.620 --> 00:40:27.120
of these neurons in your head where they look like&nbsp;
branching trees, and they make 10,000 connections&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:27.120 --> 00:40:32.820
to other neurons, these are constantly moving.&nbsp;
So imagine moving trees that are plugging and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:32.820 --> 00:40:38.040
unplugging and seeking and connecting with&nbsp;
others and so on. So anyway, that's called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:38.040 --> 00:40:43.440
brain plasticity, this constant reconfiguration.&nbsp;
And it turns out the surprise in neuroscience is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:43.440 --> 00:40:49.260
how rapidly things can start moving and changing.
And so some colleagues mine at Harvard back in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:49.800 --> 00:40:58.020
I think 2007, did this experiment where they&nbsp;
put people in fMRI, the brain scanner, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:58.020 --> 00:41:04.980
they blindfolded them tightly. And they were doing&nbsp;
things like when you're exposed to sound or touch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:04.980 --> 00:41:11.820
what's happening in the brain? And what they&nbsp;
found is that in 60 minutes, the visual system&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:11.820 --> 00:41:18.120
started responding to touch and to sound, and&nbsp;
that was really unexpected because we know that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:18.120 --> 00:41:23.580
if you go blind, your visual system will get&nbsp;
taken over, especially if you're really young.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:23.580 --> 00:41:30.300
But no one would've ever dreamt that you'd start&nbsp;
seeing the first signs of takeover in 60 minutes.&nbsp;

00:41:30.300 --> 00:41:34.440
And so that got my student and I to start&nbsp;
thinking about something right away. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:34.440 --> 00:41:38.700
we realized that dreaming has to do with the&nbsp;
rotation of the planet. And this is because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:41.040 --> 00:41:45.060
the planet rotates into darkness. So we&nbsp;
spend half our time in the dark. Obviously,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:45.060 --> 00:41:52.200
I'm not talking about electricity-blessed times,&nbsp;
but historical evolutionary times. What this means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:52.200 --> 00:41:58.560
is you can still hear, and smell, and taste, and&nbsp;
touch, in the dark, but you can't see in the dark.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:58.560 --> 00:42:03.720
And that puts the visual system at a disadvantage.&nbsp;
It's like you have been blindfolded in a sense.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:03.720 --> 00:42:11.040
And so what we realized is the visual system has&nbsp;
to fight back to keep its territory all during&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:11.040 --> 00:42:15.780
the night. And so that's what dreaming is about.&nbsp;
Every 90 minutes you've got these very specialized&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:15.780 --> 00:42:20.280
circuits that blast activity just into the visual&nbsp;
cortex. That's all that happens in dreaming is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:20.280 --> 00:42:25.500
it's going just to primary visual cortex, all&nbsp;
this random activity. And it's just to keep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:25.500 --> 00:42:30.180
it defended against takeover from its neighbors.
Tim Ferriss: To prevent the weeds of other senses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:30.180 --> 00:42:35.100
from encroaching on the walled garden of vision.
David Eagleman: Exactly. You got it. You got&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:35.100 --> 00:42:40.140
it. It's a screensaver. And so what's very&nbsp;
interesting is a lot of people have asked me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:40.140 --> 00:42:44.700
when I present this — I'm presenting this at a big&nbsp;
talk at Stanford tomorrow, by the way. And a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:44.700 --> 00:42:47.880
of people say like, "Wait, don't we already&nbsp;
know why we dream?" And the answer is, "No,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:47.880 --> 00:42:53.160
we don't know why we dream." There are various&nbsp;
hypotheses, but that doesn't mean you stop making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:53.160 --> 00:42:59.760
hypotheses, and the reason this one matters, I&nbsp;
think, is because it is the first ever one that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:59.760 --> 00:43:05.460
makes quantitative predictions across species.
So what we did is we found 25 different species&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:05.460 --> 00:43:11.700
of primates, and we looked at how plastic their&nbsp;
brains are. Some primates come out very plastic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:11.700 --> 00:43:18.780
like Homo sapiens, others, like the gray mouse&nbsp;
lemur, it's called is very not plastic. It drops&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:18.780 --> 00:43:25.800
out, it reaches puberty rapidly. It knows&nbsp;
how to walk rapidly. It's pre-programmed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:25.800 --> 00:43:28.200
if you want to think about it that way.
Tim Ferriss: What are you using as a proxy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:28.200 --> 00:43:30.780
or a measurement for plasticity?
David Eagleman: Great. So it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:30.780 --> 00:43:34.620
time to wean from their mothers, time to walking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:34.620 --> 00:43:38.820
time to puberty, things like this. So with a —
Tim Ferriss: I got it. So how much is out of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:38.820 --> 00:43:43.218
box versus how much is molding of the Play-Doh?
David Eagleman: You got it. That's exactly it.&nbsp;

00:43:43.218 --> 00:43:44.460
Tim Ferriss: Okay. Got it.
David Eagleman: And so Homo sapiens,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:44.460 --> 00:43:49.800
it's unbelievable. I mean, we're so useless&nbsp;
for so long, and that's because we've got these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:49.800 --> 00:43:54.660
extraordinary plastic brains. We drop into the&nbsp;
world half-baked, and we absorb the world around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:54.660 --> 00:43:58.140
us. We absorb the language, and the culture,&nbsp;
and all the knowledge that has come before us,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:58.140 --> 00:44:03.420
and that's why it takes us so long to develop. But&nbsp;
some of the other monkeys and apes or whatever,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:03.420 --> 00:44:07.920
they drop out, they're good to go. And if you look&nbsp;
at something like an alligator, it's just eat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:07.920 --> 00:44:13.260
run, mate. That's all it's doing. There's nothing&nbsp;
other than the pre-programming that it needs.&nbsp;

00:44:18.600 --> 00:44:22.800
Rapid eye movement sleep is what correlates&nbsp;
with dreaming. So if you look at the amount&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:22.800 --> 00:44:27.180
of REM sleep that these different animals get,&nbsp;
you find that it correlates perfectly, like a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:27.780 --> 00:44:32.204
perfect machine gun correlation.
Tim Ferriss: More REM, more plasticity.&nbsp;

00:44:32.204 --> 00:44:34.860
David Eagleman: Exactly. So if you are a creature&nbsp;
like — by the way, if you're a baby human,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:34.860 --> 00:44:42.420
you dream all the time. They have about 50 percent&nbsp;
of their sleep is REM sleep, is dream sleep. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:42.420 --> 00:44:47.640
as you get older, your brain is less plastic. And&nbsp;
by the way, you have less percentage of REM sleep.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:47.640 --> 00:44:54.360
But anyway, for an animal that's not particularly&nbsp;
plastic, it has very little dream sleep.&nbsp;

00:44:54.360 --> 00:45:00.420
Tim Ferriss: Are you aware of any counter&nbsp;
examples? Are there any species that seem to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:00.420 --> 00:45:08.100
exhibit a decent percentage of REM sleep, but&nbsp;
that one would not conclude are particularly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:08.640 --> 00:45:13.620
high plasticity outside of primates?
David Eagleman: No. I don't know any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:13.620 --> 00:45:17.880
counterexamples where they have a lot of&nbsp;
REM sleep, but I did come across one which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:17.880 --> 00:45:22.560
I thought was a real problem for the hypothesis,&nbsp;
which is elephants. Elephants have almost no REM&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:22.560 --> 00:45:26.400
sleep. They do have a little bit of REM once in&nbsp;
a while, but otherwise they don't. And of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:26.400 --> 00:45:32.220
elephants have big brains and so on, but it turns&nbsp;
out elephants only sleep one to two hours a night,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:32.220 --> 00:45:39.060
and they have excellent night vision, so they&nbsp;
don't need to keep their visual cortex protected&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:39.060 --> 00:45:44.100
against takeover in the same way that we do. So&nbsp;
that actually falls right into line in the theory.&nbsp;

00:45:44.100 --> 00:45:50.520
Tim Ferriss: I wonder if there are other animals&nbsp;
with terrible night vision that might experience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:50.520 --> 00:45:57.780
a decent percentage of their sleep as REM sleep.&nbsp;
But I mean, it's a fascinating hypothesis. I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:57.780 --> 00:46:02.435
super fascinating, and it simplifies the&nbsp;
whole thing and demystifies it if true.&nbsp;

00:46:02.435 --> 00:46:06.480
David Eagleman: Exactly. Exactly. And again, when&nbsp;
you're blasting random activity, and because we're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:06.480 --> 00:46:11.520
visual animals, we have dreams. When you get&nbsp;
activity back there, you think, "Oh, I'm seeing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:11.520 --> 00:46:14.867
them riding through a meadow. I'm talking to&nbsp;
a silver leprechaun," whatever's going on.&nbsp;

00:46:14.867 --> 00:46:20.400
Tim Ferriss: You too?
David Eagleman: What's funny about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:20.400 --> 00:46:25.020
dreams is, you wake up, and you try to explain&nbsp;
it to your spouse or whoever you're talking to,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:25.020 --> 00:46:30.900
and we layer on all this extra stuff in this&nbsp;
narrative to make it make sense. But actually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:30.900 --> 00:46:36.840
dreams are just these weird visuals that happen.
Actually, let me just mention one thing because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:36.840 --> 00:46:41.100
I always get asked about, okay, what&nbsp;
about blind people? What happens in their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:41.100 --> 00:46:44.760
dreams? Do you know the answer to that?
Tim Ferriss: I don't. I would suspect it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:44.760 --> 00:46:49.380
probably depends on when they became blind and&nbsp;
the type of blindness, but I don't know the answer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:49.380 --> 00:46:52.560
to that. So why don't you take us there?
David Eagleman: If you are a blind person,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:52.560 --> 00:46:57.900
you still have dreams at night, and that's&nbsp;
because these circuits that drive dreaming are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:57.900 --> 00:47:01.380
very ancient. You find these across the entire&nbsp;
animal kingdom, actually. So every 90 minutes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:01.380 --> 00:47:07.980
they get this random activity blasted into their&nbsp;
occipital lobe. But now that part of the brain is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:07.980 --> 00:47:15.600
no longer visual. So a blind person has dreams&nbsp;
that involve touch and hearing and so on. Like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:15.600 --> 00:47:22.440
"Oh, I was feeling my way around the living&nbsp;
room and there was a big dog in the corner,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:22.440 --> 00:47:27.000
and I got scared and I ran, but the furniture&nbsp;
was all rearranged," and whatever, but it's all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:28.020 --> 00:47:35.460
touch and hearing. It's not visual.
Tim Ferriss: I interviewed Richard Turner,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:35.460 --> 00:47:41.820
who is a very famous, close-up magician who&nbsp;
is completely blind, and he became blind at a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:41.820 --> 00:47:47.460
pretty young age. And he described his experience&nbsp;
of reality, and we have to take his word for it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:47.460 --> 00:47:52.860
of course, but highly visual. I didn't&nbsp;
ask him about his dreams, I don't think,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:54.180 --> 00:48:02.220
but really enjoyed the conversation because he&nbsp;
has the dexterity of a very well-trained sighted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:02.220 --> 00:48:07.200
person. It is nearly impossible to believe that&nbsp;
he is not fully sighted with what he can do with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:07.200 --> 00:48:11.520
his hands. It's remarkable. For people who are&nbsp;
interested, there's a documentary called Dealt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:11.520 --> 00:48:16.667
D-E-A-L-T that is fantastic about Richard Turner.
David Eagleman: Oh, I'd love to see it.&nbsp;

00:48:16.667 --> 00:48:19.980
Tim Ferriss: It's fantastic. I really&nbsp;
recommend it. There's a lot more to it than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:19.980 --> 00:48:23.520
just magic. I mean, it's mostly his life story.
David Eagleman: But I just want to point out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:23.520 --> 00:48:28.920
it's not necessarily visual. It's spatial. So he's&nbsp;
got this amazing spatial sense it sounds like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:28.920 --> 00:48:34.080
about what's where, and how to turn things,&nbsp;
and how to move things, and get things into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:34.080 --> 00:48:39.300
his pocket or whatever, but it's spatial.
Tim Ferriss: Well, he describes it in his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:39.300 --> 00:48:42.780
experience, keeping in mind that he was&nbsp;
sighted until he was something like six&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:42.780 --> 00:48:46.620
or seven years old. I can't recall&nbsp;
exactly, but if I remember correctly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:47.640 --> 00:48:56.820
different experiences have a blue or a red tone&nbsp;
to them and have different, I think he would say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:56.820 --> 00:49:03.180
visual geometric representations depending on what&nbsp;
he's interacting with, who he's interacting with.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:03.180 --> 00:49:09.180
That doesn't invalidate what you said with respect&nbsp;
to dreaming. I don't think I asked him that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:10.620 --> 00:49:17.220
but that's an area I know very little about.
David Eagleman: Oh, I'd love to get in touch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:17.220 --> 00:49:20.520
with him and ask him about his dreams.
Tim Ferriss: He's in San Antonio, Texas. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:20.520 --> 00:49:22.544
could put you in touch if you wanted.
David Eagleman: Great.&nbsp;

00:49:22.544 --> 00:49:30.000
Tim Ferriss: So let's talk about additional&nbsp;
influences. So we hit one of the big names, Crick.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:30.000 --> 00:49:37.980
Who are some of the influences that most shaped&nbsp;
where you are today and over the last say 10 years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:37.980 --> 00:49:46.200
as a scientist and just investigator of the world?
David Eagleman: I would say the ones that come to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:46.200 --> 00:49:50.520
mind are, first of all, my father who is&nbsp;
a forensic psychiatrist and just a super&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:50.520 --> 00:49:56.460
smart guy. That was a big influence of my&nbsp;
life. Then I went to graduate school, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:57.000 --> 00:50:04.860
my advisor was a guy named Read Montague, who&nbsp;
was such a terrific thinker and a great athlete,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:06.120 --> 00:50:11.340
and he was a major influence on me. And&nbsp;
the other one I guess was Carl Sagan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:11.340 --> 00:50:16.320
who I only knew through television. But that was&nbsp;
all I saw as a kid. We never watched much TV,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:16.320 --> 00:50:24.900
but I saw Cosmos on PBS and it totally influenced&nbsp;
the shape of the trajectory of my life. I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:24.900 --> 00:50:30.840
I just first of all was so turned on by all the&nbsp;
stuff he was saying and felt — it's interesting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:30.840 --> 00:50:33.900
actually, because my parents never said, "Oh,&nbsp;
you could be the next president of the United&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:33.900 --> 00:50:37.620
States." They always would say, "Oh, you&nbsp;
could be the next Carl Sagan," and that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:37.620 --> 00:50:40.380
the direction I ended up taking.
Tim Ferriss: Now I may have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:40.380 --> 00:50:43.886
missed the name. Montague?
David Eagleman: Montague, yeah.&nbsp;

00:50:43.886 --> 00:50:47.880
Tim Ferriss: Am I recalling that?&nbsp;
Montague. What was the impact that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:47.880 --> 00:50:52.860
was made and what sport, just out of curiosity?
David Eagleman: He was a decathlete. He was a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:52.860 --> 00:50:58.620
decathlete, and just unbelievably good at every&nbsp;
sport he ever tried. He was a big sprinter and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:58.620 --> 00:51:03.900
a big muscular guy. And somehow when I — look,&nbsp;
here's the thing. As an undergraduate, I majored&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:03.900 --> 00:51:10.620
in British and American literature because that&nbsp;
was my first love. And so when I decided in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:10.620 --> 00:51:15.780
my last semester of my senior year that I loved&nbsp;
neuroscience, I applied to neuroscience graduate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:15.780 --> 00:51:23.640
school, and I miraculously got in, but I wasn't&nbsp;
sure if I wanted to do a PhD in neuroscience. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:23.640 --> 00:51:27.960
didn't know what that would entail, and I figured&nbsp;
I'd end up with big Coke bottle glasses and my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:27.960 --> 00:51:32.460
hair sticking up everywhere. I didn't really know&nbsp;
what it meant to be a scientist. And so I met this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:32.460 --> 00:51:42.180
guy and he was so admirable in all the ways that&nbsp;
I wanted to be just as a killer in every way, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:42.180 --> 00:51:49.380
so smart. He was 20 IQ points ahead of everybody,&nbsp;
and also could bench press and do chin-ups better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:49.380 --> 00:51:52.500
than everybody. And so he was real impressive.
Tim Ferriss: So he broke the mold. So he showed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:52.500 --> 00:51:58.664
you that you didn't need to be resigned to the&nbsp;
Coke bottle glasses. Was that proof of concept?&nbsp;

00:51:58.664 --> 00:52:04.020
David Eagleman: Yeah, it was proof of concept,&nbsp;
and of course, the ideas, and the challenges, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:04.020 --> 00:52:11.040
whatever that it opened up my world. It opened up&nbsp;
my mind so enormously. And he was very hard on me.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:11.040 --> 00:52:16.500
I was his grad student for five years, and he was&nbsp;
a total jerk all the time. But I love the guy so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:16.500 --> 00:52:24.180
much, but he was really hard on me the whole time.
Tim Ferriss: Would you, in retrospect,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:24.180 --> 00:52:26.387
have it any other way?
David Eagleman: No.&nbsp;

00:52:26.387 --> 00:52:30.780
Tim Ferriss: Is there a version of the&nbsp;
hard that would be better or would've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:30.780 --> 00:52:34.004
potentially been better for you? Or&nbsp;
was it what you needed at the time?&nbsp;

00:52:34.004 --> 00:52:37.440
David Eagleman: I suspect this is different for&nbsp;
each person, but for me, it worked really well.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:37.440 --> 00:52:41.460
And I had a coach, a track coach in high school,&nbsp;
who was the same way. He was so mean to me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:42.360 --> 00:52:49.140
and it brought out my best behaviors that way.
Tim Ferriss: So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:49.140 --> 00:52:55.740
going from British American lit to deciding that&nbsp;
you're going to apply for a master's program&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:55.740 --> 00:53:03.060
in neuroscience seems like a very hard left&nbsp;
turn. Two questions. So you said very luckily,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:03.060 --> 00:53:08.580
something along those lines, I got in, but there's&nbsp;
got to be a little more to the story. So what was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:08.580 --> 00:53:13.380
the pitch, maybe in the form of essays or maybe&nbsp;
it's combined with standardized testing. I have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:13.380 --> 00:53:20.100
no idea what the prereqs were or the application&nbsp;
process was like, but why decide to dedicate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:20.100 --> 00:53:23.820
that time to neuroscience? Were you maybe buying&nbsp;
yourself a little bit of time to figure out what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:23.820 --> 00:53:29.760
was next? And then what do you think, if you&nbsp;
had to make an educated guess, helped you to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:29.760 --> 00:53:34.380
get in from such an seemingly unrelated field?
David Eagleman: The truth is that I was always&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:34.380 --> 00:53:38.820
studying science. And so I had tons of science&nbsp;
on my transcript. It's just that I didn't have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:38.820 --> 00:53:42.420
neuroscience in particular. And so what had&nbsp;
happened is I was really good at science&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:42.420 --> 00:53:47.040
growing up, and so everyone told me, I feel&nbsp;
like everyone gave me bad advice. They said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:47.040 --> 00:53:51.360
"Oh, you should become an actuary and do the stuff&nbsp;
with math" because I could do math or "You should&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:51.360 --> 00:53:55.800
become a waste engineer because there's a lot of&nbsp;
money in waste engineering." People had all these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:55.800 --> 00:53:59.700
proposals that were so boring and nothing&nbsp;
to do with Carl Sagan and real questions.&nbsp;

00:54:01.020 --> 00:54:05.700
So what happened is I went to college, and I&nbsp;
studied electrical engineering. I took lots&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:05.700 --> 00:54:11.160
of physics. I took lots of all kinds of things&nbsp;
and I couldn't quite find what was resonating.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:11.160 --> 00:54:16.080
I couldn't find the thing that I loved.&nbsp;
And so I ended up majoring in literature,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:16.080 --> 00:54:20.880
but I was taking these classes and it was my last&nbsp;
semester that I took a neuroscience class. And by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:20.880 --> 00:54:25.680
the way, the class I took was taught by a very old&nbsp;
professor who was using literature from let's say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:25.680 --> 00:54:31.303
the 1960s. It was a terrible neuroscience class,&nbsp;
but it didn't matter at all. I was totally hooked.&nbsp;

00:54:31.303 --> 00:54:34.740
Tim Ferriss: And so it seems like&nbsp;
you had demonstrated scientific&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:34.740 --> 00:54:40.020
chops. You're not enumerate. You can do&nbsp;
math. Was there anything else that was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:40.020 --> 00:54:44.100
key do you think, to getting into that program?
David Eagleman: Actually, in one of my philosophy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:44.100 --> 00:54:48.720
classes, we'd been assigned to write a final&nbsp;
project and I don't know, a 12-page assignment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:48.720 --> 00:54:54.600
or something. I ended up writing 108 pages on&nbsp;
the brain. And the reason I was writing so much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:54.600 --> 00:55:00.120
on it is because I went to the library and I, over&nbsp;
the course of that last semester, read every book&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:00.120 --> 00:55:03.600
that the library had on the brain, which, by the&nbsp;
way, was not that many at the time because there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:03.600 --> 00:55:08.280
just weren't that many. But I read everything.&nbsp;
So when I showed up for the graduate school&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:08.280 --> 00:55:12.840
interview, I said, "Look, I know I don't have much&nbsp;
biology on my transcript, but ask me what I know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:12.840 --> 00:55:16.920
and I'll tell you because I've read X, Y, Z, and&nbsp;
blah blah," and it worked. The problem is I don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:16.920 --> 00:55:21.347
think a kid could get into graduate school now&nbsp;
with that trajectory, and I think that's a shame.&nbsp;

00:55:21.347 --> 00:55:24.000
Tim Ferriss: Why not?
David Eagleman: Well, only because now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:24.000 --> 00:55:28.740
the competition is so high, and so if you haven't&nbsp;
rotated in labs the whole time you're an undergrad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:28.740 --> 00:55:33.227
and been doing neuroscience since you were two&nbsp;
years old and so on, then it's hard to get in.&nbsp;

00:55:33.227 --> 00:55:36.480
Tim Ferriss: And then the David Eaglemans&nbsp;
of the world end up being waste engineers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:38.400 --> 00:55:43.544
No disrespect to waste engineers, but you would&nbsp;
hope there to be a spectrum of possibilities.&nbsp;

00:55:43.544 --> 00:55:45.360
David Eagleman: Yes.
Tim Ferriss: Well, it's good that you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:45.360 --> 00:55:47.760
went through it when you did then, I suppose.
David Eagleman: Yeah. Oh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:47.760 --> 00:55:52.440
exactly. I really lucked out.
Tim Ferriss: What would you say is one of your,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:52.440 --> 00:55:56.880
for those people listening and looking at your&nbsp;
bio and watching your TED talks and think, "Man,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:56.880 --> 00:56:02.280
this guy just wakes up every morning and just&nbsp;
karate chops the universe to bend his will and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:02.280 --> 00:56:09.360
hits home runs from start to finish. I can't&nbsp;
believe it. That's not me," Favorite failures,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:09.360 --> 00:56:14.820
and you can define that any way you'd like, but to&nbsp;
frame it, failures or things that didn't work out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:14.820 --> 00:56:22.860
as you had hoped, that in retrospect taught you a&nbsp;
lot, bent your trajectory, or maybe set the stage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:22.860 --> 00:56:29.400
for later successes? Anything that comes to mind?
David Eagleman: Well, one thing that comes to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:29.400 --> 00:56:39.840
mind is I wanted to be a writer very much,&nbsp;
and so I wrote my first book called Sum.&nbsp;

00:56:39.840 --> 00:56:42.704
Tim Ferriss: Great book, by&nbsp;
the way, highly recommend.&nbsp;

00:56:42.704 --> 00:56:48.720
David Eagleman: Thank you very much. And I&nbsp;
submitted it for publication and I got rejected,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:48.720 --> 00:56:54.300
so I submitted again, I got rejected. I ended up&nbsp;
collecting a stack of rejection letters as high&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:54.300 --> 00:56:58.620
as the book. Nobody wanted to publish it. And&nbsp;
some people, most of they were form rejections,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:58.620 --> 00:57:03.240
but some people hand wrote stuff saying, "Hey, I&nbsp;
really liked this book, but I have no idea where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:03.240 --> 00:57:10.620
I would put this in the bookshelf or I don't even&nbsp;
understand what this thing is," and so that was a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:10.620 --> 00:57:16.080
failure that just kept dragging on for a very&nbsp;
long time. I had written it over seven years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:16.080 --> 00:57:21.780
and I was pretty much certain that it was never&nbsp;
actually going to get to see the light of day.&nbsp;

00:57:21.780 --> 00:57:27.300
And then finally, I just kept trying to crush it&nbsp;
from different angles, and I finally came up with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:27.300 --> 00:57:32.580
an idea of there was a particular literary agent&nbsp;
that I wanted to hit, and so I managed to figure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:32.580 --> 00:57:38.340
out three different ways to hit her so that within&nbsp;
48 hours, she was hearing from three different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:38.340 --> 00:57:43.380
people. Finally, she said, "I will take you on as&nbsp;
a hip pocket client," which just means, "You're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:43.380 --> 00:57:47.580
not really a client in mine, but I'll keep you&nbsp;
in my pocket just in case I see an opportunity."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:47.580 --> 00:57:51.840
And then she called me two days later and&nbsp;
she was incredulous and she said, "Wow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:51.840 --> 00:57:57.989
Sum just got bought," so that was the start of&nbsp;
a big thing. And Tim, I know you've read the —&nbsp;

00:57:57.989 --> 00:58:01.440
Tim Ferriss: Hold on, hold on, hold on, so before&nbsp;
you get there, you're skipping over something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:01.440 --> 00:58:06.840
really important. Two days later, seven years in&nbsp;
the making, "Sum just got bought." How does that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:06.840 --> 00:58:10.560
happen? Did she just send it out to some folks&nbsp;
and said, "Ah, I'm never going to hear back,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:10.560 --> 00:58:13.860
but fuck it. Why not? Let's try it."
David Eagleman: Exactly. Look, it took me a while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:13.860 --> 00:58:18.960
to learn that this is what literary agents are&nbsp;
about. They're the first filter and if an agent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:18.960 --> 00:58:22.380
says, "I'll take something on," then a publisher&nbsp;
will actually take a look at it, but I had been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:22.380 --> 00:58:24.524
trying to write the publishers directly.
Tim Ferriss: Right.&nbsp;

00:58:24.524 --> 00:58:29.220
David Eagleman: Tim, I know you know the book and&nbsp;
the book became an international bestseller and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:29.220 --> 00:58:34.200
pretty immediately got published in 33 languages.&nbsp;
It got turned into two operas. Brian Eno&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:34.200 --> 00:58:37.157
turned it into an opera at the Sydney Opera House.
Tim Ferriss: Didn't know that part.&nbsp;

00:58:37.157 --> 00:58:38.340
David Eagleman: Oh, okay. Yes.
Tim Ferriss: That's amazing.&nbsp;

00:58:38.340 --> 00:58:42.600
David Eagleman: Eno turned into opera and then&nbsp;
around the same time, Max Richter turned it into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:42.600 --> 00:58:49.800
an opera at the Royal Opera House of London.&nbsp;
And it finds itself back in the top 100 books&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:49.800 --> 00:58:58.800
on Amazon over the years, and so it's been such&nbsp;
a special part of my life, but the point is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:58.800 --> 00:59:04.920
it was such a long, extended failure and I'm so&nbsp;
thankful that I just kept banging on that door&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:04.920 --> 00:59:09.060
until it finally creeped open accidentally.
Tim Ferriss: What kept you going? Well, two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:09.060 --> 00:59:15.180
questions. Why write it in the first place? Not&nbsp;
to bury this, for people who don't know, Sum, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:15.180 --> 00:59:22.320
subtitle is 40 Tales from the Afterlives, that's&nbsp;
plural. And you know what? If you don't mind, I'll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:22.320 --> 00:59:29.340
just read what I put in my newsletter years ago&nbsp;
when I first read it, and I won't read all of it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:29.340 --> 00:59:33.540
but I'll mention some of it. This is what I put,&nbsp;
and you can correct me if I got anything wrong.&nbsp;

00:59:33.540 --> 00:59:37.440
"Don't let the title of this book fool you. It&nbsp;
isn't making a case for the afterlife. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:37.440 --> 00:59:40.620
this short read, easy to finish in&nbsp;
two days is a collection of 40 thought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:40.620 --> 00:59:44.520
exercises on the nature of existence, reality,&nbsp;
perception, death, pain, boredom, and more.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:45.480 --> 00:59:49.380
At the very least, it should make you appreciate&nbsp;
your own life, warts and all, much more."&nbsp;

00:59:49.380 --> 00:59:54.060
And then in the official book description, "In&nbsp;
one afterlife, you may find God is the size of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:54.060 --> 00:59:58.020
microbe and unaware of your existence. In another&nbsp;
version, you work as a background character in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:58.020 --> 01:00:01.920
other people's dreams or you may find that God&nbsp;
is a married couple or that the universe is&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:01.920 --> 01:00:05.700
running backward or that you're forced to live out&nbsp;
your afterlife with annoying versions of who you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:05.700 --> 01:00:16.800
could have been." Why write this book and what&nbsp;
gave you the gumption, the persistence to keep&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:16.800 --> 01:00:21.480
going? Because that doesn't always happen, to be&nbsp;
clear. There are plenty of high-achieving people&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:21.480 --> 01:00:29.580
who start books and finish them, get rejected,&nbsp;
and after a while, they stop. Why did you end&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:29.580 --> 01:00:35.220
up hitting this agent from multiple angles? Why&nbsp;
the book and then why did you end up continuing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:35.220 --> 01:00:38.760
and hitting this agent from multiple angles?
David Eagleman: Why the book actually started&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:38.760 --> 01:00:44.220
when I was, I think, 11 years old and I&nbsp;
ended up talking to a rabbi and I said, "Hey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:44.220 --> 01:00:49.740
I've never understood, what is the Jewish view of&nbsp;
the afterlife?" And he said, "Well, you ask three&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:49.740 --> 01:00:53.580
Jews, you'll get four opinions," and I thought&nbsp;
that was so amazing because I'd never heard&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:53.580 --> 01:00:59.640
someone say something like that before. That was&nbsp;
actually the seed of the book and that's the why.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:59.640 --> 01:01:05.940
Anyway, and then again, as a lover of literature&nbsp;
and a writer, I found this concept of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:05.940 --> 01:01:11.700
afterlife such a great backdrop upon which to ask&nbsp;
all kinds of questions. And as you know from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:11.700 --> 01:01:19.980
book, it's funny and there's pathos and — anyway.
I do know exactly why I kept going and that was&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:19.980 --> 01:01:26.040
because of a 16-year-old kid that I met when I&nbsp;
was at the Salk Institute with Francis Crick,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:26.040 --> 01:01:30.180
actually. What happened is there was a high school&nbsp;
science day and these kids came over and I talked&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:30.180 --> 01:01:34.500
to them about what I was doing and one of the&nbsp;
kids came up to me afterwards and said, "Can&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:34.500 --> 01:01:38.580
I work with you? This is what I want to do. Can I&nbsp;
work with you?" And I said, "Sure, write down your&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:39.300 --> 01:01:43.800
phone number on the dry erase board here."&nbsp;
And he did. And the next day, the dry erase&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:43.800 --> 01:01:50.040
board got erased and so I forgot about the kid.&nbsp;
Then I get a voicemail from him saying, "Hey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:50.040 --> 01:01:53.580
I'm the kid, blah, blah, blah, blah, blah." Okay.&nbsp;
And then I thought, "Oh, I've got to call this guy&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:53.580 --> 01:01:57.720
back" and then I forgot. And then I get a letter&nbsp;
from his high school teacher saying, "Hey, this&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:57.720 --> 01:02:01.140
is just a letter to recommend the kid." Then I get&nbsp;
called from him again. Then I get an email and I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:01.140 --> 01:02:08.460
thought, "Wait a minute. I don't want to miss this&nbsp;
kid. This kid's amazing." He ended up working in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:08.460 --> 01:02:14.498
my lab and for the last 16 years has been my&nbsp;
closest collaborator. His name is Don Vaughn.&nbsp;

01:02:14.498 --> 01:02:17.100
Tim Ferriss: Amazing. That's incredible.
David Eagleman: And I call this the Don Vaughn&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:17.100 --> 01:02:21.900
method of just hitting someone over and over from&nbsp;
different angles to make sure that they don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:21.900 --> 01:02:28.500
forget — I was so impressed by this, because I was&nbsp;
busy and I couldn't remember to call him back and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:28.500 --> 01:02:33.720
whenever, and so I was really impressed with this,&nbsp;
and so when I was trying to get Sum published at&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:33.720 --> 01:02:38.160
the end there, I thought explicitly, "I'm going&nbsp;
to do the Don Vaughn method and I'm going to hit&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:38.160 --> 01:02:42.284
this agent from every angle that I can."
Tim Ferriss: That's incredible. And it worked.&nbsp;

01:02:42.284 --> 01:02:44.280
David Eagleman: Yeah, it worked.
Tim Ferriss: That's remarkable.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:44.280 --> 01:02:50.160
Speaking of literature, I think this is from The&nbsp;
New York Times a long time ago, but you've said&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:50.160 --> 01:02:58.380
that Gabriel García Márquez, Italo Calvino, Jorge&nbsp;
Luis Borges, Toni Morrison, and William Faulkner&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:58.380 --> 01:03:05.940
are among some of your literary influences, maybe&nbsp;
heroes. Feel free to add more, but I'm curious for&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:05.940 --> 01:03:10.080
someone who has not read, let's assume someone&nbsp;
hasn't read anything from any of these writers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:11.100 --> 01:03:16.260
Are there two or three books that come to&nbsp;
mind that you might suggest people start with?&nbsp;

01:03:16.260 --> 01:03:26.160
David Eagleman: Yeah. Italo Calvino's Invisible&nbsp;
Cities is an extraordinary book about Marco Polo&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:26.160 --> 01:03:33.300
talking to the Great Khan, Kublai Khan, about&nbsp;
different cities in the Khan's Empire, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:33.300 --> 01:03:37.440
it's magical realism. And by the way, all five of&nbsp;
those authors that you named, that I guess I named&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:37.440 --> 01:03:44.400
back in that article in 2011, all five of them,&nbsp;
they're all magical realism, which means it's not&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:44.400 --> 01:03:50.580
just writing about the day-to-day stuff, but just&nbsp;
feeling free to go off in crazy directions about&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:50.580 --> 01:03:56.100
things that shouldn't be possible and they just&nbsp;
make it possible. I've always loved that style&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:56.100 --> 01:04:02.160
of writing. All five of those are really still my&nbsp;
favorites all these years later. A Hundred Years&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:02.160 --> 01:04:09.540
of Solitude, if someone hasn't read that, Borges'&nbsp;
Labyrinths, Toni Morrison's Beloved, essentially&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:09.540 --> 01:04:14.040
anything by Faulkner, The Bear is a great one.
Tim Ferriss: I'll add one more to the list for&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:14.040 --> 01:04:20.220
people who are looking for something very strange&nbsp;
and it's also very short, but I think it's The&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:20.220 --> 01:04:26.700
Baron in the Trees by Italo Calvino, which is&nbsp;
about basically a young boy who gets in a fight&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:26.700 --> 01:04:32.280
with his father, comes from this esteemed&nbsp;
family, escapes into the tree in his yard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:32.280 --> 01:04:36.960
and never comes down for the rest of his life,&nbsp;
and he builds this entire life in the trees in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:36.960 --> 01:04:44.280
the canopy. It's about as strange as it sounds.
You mentioned the word hypothesis has come up a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:44.280 --> 01:04:51.540
bunch in this conversation and you've said&nbsp;
in, I think, it was Smithsonian Magazine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:52.500 --> 01:04:58.020
that you encourage your son to do&nbsp;
hypothesis testing. Could you speak to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:58.020 --> 01:05:02.880
this and just explain what that might look like?
David Eagleman: Oh, yeah. It's anytime he says,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:02.880 --> 01:05:06.960
"Hey, Dad, how does this work?" And I've got&nbsp;
a daughter now, too, and so you'll say, "Hey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:06.960 --> 01:05:11.340
how does this work?" I say, "Well, how do you&nbsp;
think it works?" So they come up with an idea&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:11.340 --> 01:05:13.860
and I say, "Okay, cool. Can you come up with&nbsp;
another idea?" They come up with another. "Okay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:13.860 --> 01:05:18.240
great. Now how would we test that? How would you&nbsp;
distinguish those two ideas?" That's what it is to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:18.240 --> 01:05:23.580
come up with something on your own and figure out&nbsp;
how to test it, because whether or not they become&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:23.580 --> 01:05:29.100
scientists, I mean, they probably won't, for all I&nbsp;
know, but definitely want them to be good thinkers&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:29.100 --> 01:05:34.560
and be able to figure things out so they're&nbsp;
not dependent on dogma or what their neighbor&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:34.560 --> 01:05:39.240
tells them is the answer, that sort of thing.
Tim Ferriss: If you were giving advice to, say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:39.240 --> 01:05:45.480
let's put you back in the realm of academia,&nbsp;
so you've got a super promising postdoc, one&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:45.480 --> 01:05:50.760
of the smartest you've ever met, maybe has some&nbsp;
IP that has spun out or something that has spun&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:50.760 --> 01:05:54.780
out into a full-time, I shouldn't say full-time,&nbsp;
for-profit company, and they come to you to ask&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:54.780 --> 01:06:01.800
for time management or advice, prioritization&nbsp;
advice, just general life management and advice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:01.800 --> 01:06:05.340
because they fear that they're going to get&nbsp;
overwhelmed. They look at your life. They say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:05.340 --> 01:06:09.780
"This is the guy who can do anything. He's&nbsp;
the decathlete of business and science,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:10.980 --> 01:06:17.100
and put in that situation, what advice might&nbsp;
you give them or what might you say to them?&nbsp;

01:06:17.100 --> 01:06:23.940
David Eagleman: I would only be able to give what&nbsp;
I do, and that is the lazy Susan method. Do you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:23.940 --> 01:06:24.930
know what lazy Susan is?
Tim Ferriss: I do.&nbsp;

01:06:24.930 --> 01:06:27.120
David Eagleman: It's the circular&nbsp;
thing that sits in the middle of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:27.120 --> 01:06:28.740
table. Okay. Not everyone knows what this is —
Tim Ferriss: You can spin it around to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:28.740 --> 01:06:31.560
different dishes around the table.
David Eagleman: Exactly. It turns out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:31.560 --> 01:06:36.180
I learned a long time ago that Walt Whitman&nbsp;
would do this with his writing projects. Each&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:36.180 --> 01:06:40.980
segment in the lazy Susan, he'd have a different&nbsp;
writing project, and he would work on something&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:40.980 --> 01:06:44.760
until he was slowing down, and then he'd spin&nbsp;
it and randomly pick a different project and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:44.760 --> 01:06:49.080
work on that, and that is precisely what I do. I&nbsp;
loved seeing that story about him because that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:49.080 --> 01:06:55.320
my life. I work on whatever I am maximally&nbsp;
resonating with at that moment and as soon as&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:55.320 --> 01:06:59.700
I start slowing down or get stuck, I just jump to&nbsp;
a different project. And when I come back to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:00.240 --> 01:07:04.980
thing I had been working on, I'm more&nbsp;
refreshed in a sense, or I've thought&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:04.980 --> 01:07:09.420
of a new idea about it, that kind of thing.
Tim Ferriss: Where do you keep this lazy Susan?&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:09.420 --> 01:07:13.560
Presumably, it's not a physical lazy Susan.
David Eagleman: Not physical, just in my&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:13.560 --> 01:07:19.260
prefrontal cortex. Yeah. Spin it around there.
Tim Ferriss: If we could take a closer look&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:19.260 --> 01:07:21.780
at this prefrontal lazy Susan for a second,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:22.500 --> 01:07:29.220
let's just take this upcoming week. We're&nbsp;
talking on a Monday and I don't know what&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:29.220 --> 01:07:32.820
your calendar looks like. Maybe you could just&nbsp;
describe the architecture of your week a little&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:32.820 --> 01:07:36.900
bit. That would actually be very helpful.
David Eagleman: A lot of it is work meetings&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:36.900 --> 01:07:42.840
with my company, Neosensory. Some of&nbsp;
it is meetings with VCs about that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:43.860 --> 01:07:48.360
Then I have various lectures I'm giving. It&nbsp;
happens that this week, several of them are&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:48.360 --> 01:07:56.100
virtual lectures, so that's a lot easier for me.&nbsp;
Then working on how to do B2B stuff, business&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:56.100 --> 01:08:03.000
to business stuff, because we're now selling the&nbsp;
wristband not only to customers, but directly to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:03.000 --> 01:08:08.100
big businesses with big warehouses for their deaf&nbsp;
employees. But then, let's see, I'm giving a talk&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:08.940 --> 01:08:16.020
to a bunch of Stanford students. Then I run&nbsp;
the Center for Science and Law, which is where&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:16.020 --> 01:08:19.140
neuroscience and the legal system intersects,&nbsp;
so I'm having some meetings about that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:20.100 --> 01:08:27.300
I also just finished a book about, okay, this is a&nbsp;
little embarrassing because it's so unusual. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:27.300 --> 01:08:30.660
so different than anything else I've written,&nbsp;
but it's a young adult book or middle grade,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:31.680 --> 01:08:37.080
but it's about a brother and sister team who solve&nbsp;
mysteries like Hardy Boys or Encyclopedia Brown,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:37.080 --> 01:08:40.860
but all the mysteries have to do with the&nbsp;
brain, so it's really, I think, educational&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:40.860 --> 01:08:45.480
and lovely. But anyway, so I'm having a meeting&nbsp;
about that, and then it happens at tomorrow's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:45.480 --> 01:08:47.864
my birthday, so unfortunately —
Tim Ferriss: Happy birthday.&nbsp;

01:08:47.864 --> 01:08:50.760
David Eagleman: Thank you. Unfortunately, I'm&nbsp;
looking at my calendar, it turns out I have&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:50.760 --> 01:08:55.032
zero time on my calendar to do anything&nbsp;
that would resemble birthdayness, but —&nbsp;

01:08:55.031 --> 01:08:58.259
Tim Ferriss: Maybe that is the&nbsp;
quintessential David Eagleman&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:58.260 --> 01:09:03.000
birthday is a calendar that's completely full.
David Eagleman: That sucks. Then I teach&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:03.000 --> 01:09:08.580
neural law at Stanford on Thursday. Then&nbsp;
I'm having dinner with a startup that I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:08.580 --> 01:09:13.500
advising. Then I'm doing my podcast, actually,&nbsp;
twice this week on Tuesday and on Friday. I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:13.500 --> 01:09:18.420
doing a three-hour recording session.
Tim Ferriss: Okay. This is very helpful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:19.200 --> 01:09:25.740
a glimpse behind the curtain, and what I'd love&nbsp;
to know is if you had to pick something in there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:27.000 --> 01:09:33.060
to use this hypothetical with the lazy Susan, is&nbsp;
there anything that you could walk us through?&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:33.060 --> 01:09:36.420
Let's say you're getting ready to do the podcast.&nbsp;
You're prepping for hours and you're just like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:36.420 --> 01:09:43.560
"You know what? I am fucking exhausted and&nbsp;
it's my birthday. I don't want to be doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:43.560 --> 01:09:48.180
this right now." Is that the type of case that&nbsp;
you might have, where then you would switch to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:48.180 --> 01:09:52.920
something else, in which case, which is not to&nbsp;
throw the podcast under the bus. I'm trying to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:52.920 --> 01:09:56.520
find something where you're not fully obligated&nbsp;
to other people, because I imagine when you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:56.520 --> 01:10:01.860
in a Zoom meeting, if you're 30 minutes into 60&nbsp;
minutes, you're probably disinclined to be like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:01.860 --> 01:10:06.420
"You know what, guys? My heart's not in it. I'm&nbsp;
going to focus on my new young adult book that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:06.420 --> 01:10:12.720
I'm working on." Is there anything in this week&nbsp;
where you might be able to hypothetically describe&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:12.720 --> 01:10:15.720
how the lazy Susan might work?
David Eagleman: Unfortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:15.720 --> 01:10:20.700
the lazy Susan can only get spun on the weekends&nbsp;
or something, when there's opportunity to think.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:20.700 --> 01:10:25.620
This week happens to be a pretty tough one, so&nbsp;
I'm constrained in every moment and I just have&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:25.620 --> 01:10:30.660
to perform. The lazy Susan comes in when I say,&nbsp;
"Wow, I've got a four-hour chunk and I'm going&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:30.660 --> 01:10:35.200
to sit down at IHOP." By the way, I've written all&nbsp;
my books at IHOPs. I don't know why, but I love —&nbsp;

01:10:35.200 --> 01:10:36.360
Tim Ferriss: I love that.
David Eagleman: Yeah. It's bottomless&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:36.360 --> 01:10:41.340
coffee and it's quiet. It's not like a Starbucks,&nbsp;
where attractive people are walking in and out&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:41.340 --> 01:10:45.540
all the time, whatever. It's just quiet and&nbsp;
boring and you can just drink coffee and write,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:45.540 --> 01:10:50.340
but that's where I spin it, because what happens&nbsp;
is I'll work on something and then I'll say, "Oh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:50.340 --> 01:10:55.620
yeah. I really want to get to this other thing,"&nbsp;
and so I'll spin it and do the other thing.&nbsp;

01:10:55.620 --> 01:11:03.540
Tim Ferriss: That makes sense. Do you think&nbsp;
about how much of that solo time you want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:03.540 --> 01:11:08.040
block out on a weekly, monthly, quarterly,&nbsp;
annual basis, anything along those lines,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:08.040 --> 01:11:11.220
because I would imagine otherwise,&nbsp;
it's a slippery slope and you end up&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:12.000 --> 01:11:19.440
committed to a large chunk of your waking life&nbsp;
involving other people, so there's a social&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:20.700 --> 01:11:26.520
pressure expectation that you will not cancel or&nbsp;
move things around, so it maybe creates a degree&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:26.520 --> 01:11:30.660
of inflexibility. Do you think about that?
David Eagleman: Yes, I do think about that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:30.660 --> 01:11:38.580
all the time. And I really admire, Tim,&nbsp;
your 4-Hour Workweek as an example. I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:38.580 --> 01:11:44.807
working on the 400-hour work week and it sucks&nbsp;
because I'm totally constrained on all fronts.&nbsp;

01:11:44.807 --> 01:11:48.360
Tim Ferriss: You seem to be having a good time.
David Eagleman: I'm having a good time and it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:48.360 --> 01:11:50.520
my own fault and my wife accuses me of — I choose&nbsp;
it, obviously. The thing is I can't resist new,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:50.520 --> 01:11:55.680
shiny objects, so every time some student&nbsp;
says, "Hey, I want to start a company. Will&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:55.680 --> 01:11:58.980
you help me and be an advisor?" I'm like,&nbsp;
"Heck, yeah. That sounds amazing," and I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:58.980 --> 01:12:03.300
do that. Every time I come up with a new idea&nbsp;
for a screenplay or book or whatever, I'm like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:03.300 --> 01:12:07.500
"That's the greatest idea," and I jump in on it.
Tim Ferriss: You may not want to start a venture&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:07.500 --> 01:12:13.080
capital firm. You'll end up saying yes to&nbsp;
everything. Actually, one of my mentors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:13.080 --> 01:12:18.420
Ed Zschau, incredible entrepreneur, also was a&nbsp;
competitive figure skater, then a congressman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:18.420 --> 01:12:22.980
and took a couple of companies public. He taught&nbsp;
computer science. I think he was the first or&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:22.980 --> 01:12:29.580
maybe the second to ever teach computer science&nbsp;
at Stanford and he tried to become a venture&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:29.580 --> 01:12:32.580
capitalist. He did become one at one point and&nbsp;
he realized, "I'm not any good at this. I get&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:32.580 --> 01:12:34.247
too excited." I'm just saying.
David Eagleman: Nice.&nbsp;

01:12:34.247 --> 01:12:43.140
Tim Ferriss: Let's talk about Livewired. And this&nbsp;
question was cued for me, in a way, by Annaka&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:43.140 --> 01:12:49.680
Harris, who's an incredible writer, who's written&nbsp;
a lot on consciousness. The topic we covered at&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:49.680 --> 01:12:55.226
the very beginning of this conversation on some&nbsp;
level, infotropism. Am I getting this term right?&nbsp;

01:12:55.226 --> 01:12:58.980
David Eagleman: Yeah.
Tim Ferriss: Could you walk us through the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:58.980 --> 01:13:06.638
premise of Livewired, the reason for writing it,&nbsp;
and then unpack infotropism, if you wouldn't mind?&nbsp;

01:13:06.638 --> 01:13:11.520
David Eagleman: Yeah. Okay, so Livewired in&nbsp;
general is about this issue of how you can't&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:11.520 --> 01:13:15.660
really think about the brain like hardware or&nbsp;
software, but instead it's what I call liveware,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:15.660 --> 01:13:19.080
where it's reconfiguring itself all the&nbsp;
time, this thing I mentioned earlier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:19.080 --> 01:13:25.080
brain plasticity. There's something like 30,000&nbsp;
papers a year coming out on brain plasticity now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:25.080 --> 01:13:32.820
but I wrote this book to synthesize and build the&nbsp;
framework for understanding what it is for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:32.820 --> 01:13:41.100
brain to absorb the world around it and do that.&nbsp;
What I surfaced that way were some principles&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:42.060 --> 01:13:47.400
that were interesting to me and one of them is&nbsp;
about, infotropism is a term that I coined about&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:48.300 --> 01:13:51.780
what the brain is trying to do at all&nbsp;
times. It's reconfiguring its circuitry&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:51.780 --> 01:13:56.580
so that it can maximize the information&nbsp;
that it brings in from the world.&nbsp;

01:13:56.580 --> 01:13:59.940
Just as an example, if&nbsp;
you're watching a waterfall,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:59.940 --> 01:14:02.700
we've probably all had the experience.&nbsp;
You're staring at a waterfall for a while,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:02.700 --> 01:14:06.240
and then you look over to the rocks of trees at&nbsp;
the side and it looks like everything's moving&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:06.240 --> 01:14:10.500
up. This is called the motion aftereffect, where&nbsp;
you see motion moving in the opposite direction.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:12.300 --> 01:14:16.860
I make a long argument, but I'll just say in a&nbsp;
sentence here that what the brain's trying to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:16.860 --> 01:14:23.820
do is say, "Okay, I'm going to reset my zero. I'm&nbsp;
going to recalibrate so that I can see if there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:23.820 --> 01:14:27.780
more information here because I don't want to&nbsp;
just…" Essentially what it would be trying to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:27.780 --> 01:14:31.860
do is cancel out the downward motion because it&nbsp;
says, "Okay, there's lots of that, but there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:31.860 --> 01:14:36.120
no new information there, so I'm trying to&nbsp;
cancel that out," and it does it by having&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:36.120 --> 01:14:39.720
this upward motion. There's more sophisticated&nbsp;
arguments about how it does this neurally.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:40.740 --> 01:14:44.760
It's doing that to maximize the information.
Same if I change the color of the lights in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:44.760 --> 01:14:52.560
room, let's say I make it all red. Your brain will&nbsp;
quickly stop seeing the red and it will adjust&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:52.560 --> 01:14:57.720
itself so that it's seeing other things, so it can&nbsp;
maximize what it's pulling out at all times. Of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:57.720 --> 01:15:02.040
course, you know this. If you put on, let's say,&nbsp;
yellow sunglasses or something, everything looks&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:02.040 --> 01:15:07.260
normal after a moment until you take them off&nbsp;
again. Anyway, this is a general thing that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:07.260 --> 01:15:13.020
brain's always trying to do, is say, "How can I&nbsp;
pull the most useful information out of the world&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:13.020 --> 01:15:19.260
by recalibrating everything else around me?"
Tim Ferriss: What was your hope in publishing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:19.260 --> 01:15:24.540
that book? What would you like people&nbsp;
to take from that or what would you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:24.540 --> 01:15:30.840
like people to grab and run with from that?
David Eagleman: I think the notion of how&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:30.840 --> 01:15:37.440
plastic the brain actually is is something that's&nbsp;
totally underappreciated. There's an idea that I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:37.440 --> 01:15:40.740
think many people have, which is, "Oh, you're&nbsp;
just born with the brain. The brain unpacks&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:40.740 --> 01:15:45.180
and then you're who you are," but really, we&nbsp;
are totally functions of our culture and our&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:45.180 --> 01:15:50.100
society. And one of the things that's amazing&nbsp;
to me is, imagine, Tim, that you and I were born&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:50.820 --> 01:15:56.640
10,000 years ago, exactly the same DNA, but&nbsp;
we wouldn't be anything like what we are&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:56.640 --> 01:16:02.940
now. Even though with the same DNA we might look&nbsp;
vaguely like we do now, but my God, the culture,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:02.940 --> 01:16:07.500
everything we're pulling in would be so different.&nbsp;
And that's what actually shapes a human being.&nbsp;

01:16:08.280 --> 01:16:14.640
And I actually started off the book with a&nbsp;
quotation from Heidegger who says — oh, it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:14.640 --> 01:16:21.300
something like, "Every man begins life as 10,000&nbsp;
men and dies as a single one." And what that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:21.300 --> 01:16:28.440
means is you've got all this potential, all these&nbsp;
directions you could go in your life, but as you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:28.440 --> 01:16:34.080
move through life and you make choices and stuff&nbsp;
happens to you, you end up as exactly who you are.&nbsp;

01:16:34.080 --> 01:16:39.840
Tim Ferriss: Yeah. I'm looking at Martin&nbsp;
Heidegger. I just wanted to find it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:39.840 --> 01:16:44.460
Tell me if this sounds right to you.&nbsp;
"Anyone can achieve their fullest potential.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:45.240 --> 01:16:49.020
Who we are might be predetermined, but the path&nbsp;
we follow is always of our own choosing. We should&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:49.020 --> 01:16:53.940
never allow our fears or the expectations of&nbsp;
others to set the frontiers of our destiny." And&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:53.940 --> 01:17:00.120
then, there's the popup to continue with Facebook.&nbsp;
Thank you, Goodreads. They come by. "Your destiny&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:00.120 --> 01:17:05.100
can't be changed, but it can be challenged. Every&nbsp;
man is born as many men and dies as a single one."&nbsp;

01:17:05.100 --> 01:17:11.820
David Eagleman: Right, right, right. Nice.
Tim Ferriss: So let me ask you then about&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:15.660 --> 01:17:24.780
just a thought exercise. If we look through the&nbsp;
telescope into the future or the probably the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:24.780 --> 01:17:29.640
scrying ball. Look into the scrying ball and try&nbsp;
to figure out what might be coming down the pike,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:29.640 --> 01:17:35.100
knowing what you know and having the breadth&nbsp;
of exposure that you have within science and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:35.100 --> 01:17:41.760
outside of science, let's just say in the next 10&nbsp;
years, the next decade, what current assumptions&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:41.760 --> 01:17:50.460
about the brain perception or reality do you think&nbsp;
potentially going to be overturned or that might&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:50.460 --> 01:17:54.360
be ripe for challenging. Is there anything?
David Eagleman: I think the main thing has to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:54.360 --> 01:18:00.420
do with technology as opposed to theoretical&nbsp;
ideas. So, what I mean is we've got plenty&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:00.420 --> 01:18:03.840
of theoretical hypotheses all about how&nbsp;
the brain might be working and so on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:03.840 --> 01:18:08.700
but we actually don't have any good technology for&nbsp;
measuring what's going on inside the brain. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:09.480 --> 01:18:14.220
we've got brain imaging where you see these&nbsp;
fancy images of brains with the false color&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:14.220 --> 01:18:19.680
images on it and so on, but it's really crude.&nbsp;
That's fMRI. That's just measuring where the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:19.680 --> 01:18:25.320
blood flow goes, and you're measuring cubes&nbsp;
of millions and millions of neurons at once,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:25.920 --> 01:18:30.180
and all you can say is, "Oh, there was some&nbsp;
activity there somewhere in that cube."&nbsp;

01:18:30.180 --> 01:18:38.520
So, what we really need is a technology that&nbsp;
allows us to measure the spiking activity of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:38.520 --> 01:18:45.000
every neuron in the brain, let's say 86 billion&nbsp;
neurons all at once in real time, ideally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:45.000 --> 01:18:50.220
and then, do massive computational analysis of&nbsp;
that to understand what is the language of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:50.220 --> 01:18:56.220
brain. Somehow, we know it involves these spikes,&nbsp;
but we don't crack the neural code yet. So, in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:56.220 --> 01:19:02.220
the same way that Francis Crick and James Watson&nbsp;
cracked the genetic code, "Oh, it's ACTG," and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:02.220 --> 01:19:06.060
whole thing is just about keeping the order of the&nbsp;
base pairs and all the rest is just housekeeping.&nbsp;

01:19:07.080 --> 01:19:09.420
We haven't figured out what&nbsp;
the neural code is yet. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:09.420 --> 01:19:13.500
what we're facing — I don't know if it'll be 10&nbsp;
years, hopefully, maybe it'll be 20 or 100, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:14.160 --> 01:19:20.160
what we're facing is a giant leap in understanding&nbsp;
that we know is coming, but we just don't have the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:20.160 --> 01:19:26.580
trick cracked yet. So that's one thing.
Was your question about any kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:26.580 --> 01:19:29.700
invention or neuroscience in particular?
Tim Ferriss: It could be broader than&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:29.700 --> 01:19:35.880
neuroscience, but I was thinking related&nbsp;
to the brain, the mind perception, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:35.880 --> 01:19:41.940
really anything that checks any of those boxes,&nbsp;
but we can go beyond that. What I find so&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:41.940 --> 01:19:47.640
mesmerizing and frustrating about humans, and of&nbsp;
course, I'm one of those, so I put myself in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:47.640 --> 01:19:55.560
same group, it's just how certain we are at so&nbsp;
many points. And I know you have quoted Voltaire&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:55.560 --> 01:19:59.640
before saying "Uncertainty is an uncomfortable&nbsp;
position, but certainty is an absurd one."&nbsp;

01:19:59.640 --> 01:20:04.320
But if you look back, especially, in the&nbsp;
history of medicine, let's just say at&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:04.320 --> 01:20:10.260
how certain humans are including — let's just&nbsp;
call them top specialists in any given field&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:11.220 --> 01:20:16.320
that what they know to be true or think to be&nbsp;
true is true. And then, it gets overturned,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:16.320 --> 01:20:21.060
and then, it gets overturned again, and then, it&nbsp;
gets overturned again, which is not to create a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:21.060 --> 01:20:25.020
relativism that we can't know anything. I&nbsp;
think that's a learned hopelessness that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:25.020 --> 01:20:31.500
not very helpful. But I'm wondering, as someone&nbsp;
who has experience as a practitioner of science,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:31.500 --> 01:20:38.820
also an observer of science, a communicator&nbsp;
of science, a deep thinker on these subjects,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:39.360 --> 01:20:48.600
what might be worth stress testing or things&nbsp;
that we currently take to be true that may&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:48.600 --> 01:20:52.200
in fact not be true. Doesn't need to&nbsp;
be contained in neuroscience though.&nbsp;

01:20:52.200 --> 01:20:56.880
David Eagleman: This is the beauty of science is&nbsp;
that it's always willing to knock down its own&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:56.880 --> 01:21:01.680
walls. And so, this is what the scientific&nbsp;
method is about, and it's the most special&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:01.680 --> 01:21:06.780
thing that humans have ever done because&nbsp;
everything else is about how do I establish&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:07.440 --> 01:21:13.260
my throne? And I set this up, and no one can&nbsp;
question this, but there's nothing in science&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:13.260 --> 01:21:19.680
that isn't vulnerable to getting knocked off its&nbsp;
pedestal. And so, in fact, much earlier in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:19.680 --> 01:21:23.700
podcast when you asked me about maybe view of&nbsp;
consciousness, whether it's emergent property&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:23.700 --> 01:21:26.940
or whether consciousness is a fundamental, who&nbsp;
the heck knows? And I think as you get older&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:26.940 --> 01:21:31.380
and more mature as a scientist, you feel like,&nbsp;
"Okay, I don't know. I don't know. We'll see.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:31.380 --> 01:21:36.660
We'll do experiments. We'll figure it out." But&nbsp;
there's no need to pretend certainty on things.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:36.660 --> 01:21:40.980
And in fact, that's always driven me crazy.
And one thing I — I walked into a bookstore.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:40.980 --> 01:21:45.240
This is many years ago. About the time that Sum&nbsp;
came out, actually. Walked into a bookstore and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:45.240 --> 01:21:51.300
there was a table of books on the front, and&nbsp;
one was by religious fundamentalists, one pile,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:51.300 --> 01:21:56.760
and the other pile was by the neo atheists.&nbsp;
And the table was set up as this battle&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:56.760 --> 01:22:03.180
between these. But as you know from reading Sum,&nbsp;
it's all about shining a flashlight around the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:03.180 --> 01:22:07.560
possibility space. It's all about saying, "God,&nbsp;
who knows what's going on here? Let's just make&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:07.560 --> 01:22:16.020
up a bunch of stories. Let's make up 40 stories,&nbsp;
and we can make up 400 more." And so, that idea,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:16.020 --> 01:22:21.600
you may know this, but years ago I proposed this&nbsp;
new movement called Possibilianism, which really&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:21.600 --> 01:22:26.820
cut on actually is still moving around the planet.
Well, but the idea with Possibilianism is&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:26.820 --> 01:22:31.620
avoiding, committing to something with&nbsp;
certainty if you don't need to. And really,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:31.620 --> 01:22:36.240
this is just an expression of the scientific&nbsp;
temperament. This science is always one say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:36.240 --> 01:22:42.000
"Look. What we can have is the weight of&nbsp;
evidence at any given time." We can say, "Oh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:42.000 --> 01:22:46.440
that supports this more than this. Okay, great."&nbsp;
So, we'll provisionally believe this to be true,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:47.580 --> 01:22:54.480
but it's not about pretending certainty when we&nbsp;
don't have enough data. And when it comes to some&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:54.480 --> 01:22:59.280
fundamental questions like our existence here,&nbsp;
what the heck? I mean, we don't have any data on&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:59.280 --> 01:23:04.020
that. We don't have enough to know if we should&nbsp;
be. I mean, certainly religion, we have enough&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:04.020 --> 01:23:09.720
data to know that all those biblical stories are&nbsp;
completely made up and wrong in a thousand ways,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:09.720 --> 01:23:13.980
but we certainly don't have enough evidence to&nbsp;
say, "Oh, therefore there's nothing interesting&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:13.980 --> 01:23:21.660
in the cosmos besides us." I mean, who knows?
Tim Ferriss: In my very limited muggle experience&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:21.660 --> 01:23:29.820
of science, and life, really, the best people.&nbsp;
They're true outliers in terms of intellectual&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:29.820 --> 01:23:35.940
horsepower and performance say, "I don't know&nbsp;
a lot." And I remember going on a walk with&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:35.940 --> 01:23:40.680
someone recently and they wanted to get my two&nbsp;
cents back channel reference check on someone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:41.400 --> 01:23:50.700
and my commentary was pretty simple. It was, well,&nbsp;
clearly very smart in a lot of ways, but almost no&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:50.700 --> 01:23:56.280
low-conviction statements. And that makes me a&nbsp;
little nervous. Everything is a high-conviction&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:56.280 --> 01:24:02.880
statement, and that gives me some pause.
So, you're right. I think the reserving&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:02.880 --> 01:24:09.780
the space for uncertainty and to the extent&nbsp;
possible for me at least, trying to find that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:10.380 --> 01:24:16.320
exciting, the fact that there's still so much&nbsp;
unmapped territory as opposed to debilitating.&nbsp;

01:24:17.760 --> 01:24:20.940
David, I'm going to ask you a question&nbsp;
that is sometimes a terrible question,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:20.940 --> 01:24:24.000
but I'll throw it out there anyway and&nbsp;
we can delete it if it doesn't pan out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:24.000 --> 01:24:30.300
which is the metaphorical billboard question,&nbsp;
if you could put anything on a billboard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:30.300 --> 01:24:35.880
non-commercial, of course, to get out to&nbsp;
hundreds of millions or billions of people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:35.880 --> 01:24:40.620
could be a quote, could be a word, could&nbsp;
be an image, could be someone else's quote,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:41.160 --> 01:24:45.840
anything at all. Does anything come to mind?
David Eagleman: I think I would put a billboard&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:46.680 --> 01:24:52.920
next to an elementary school, or maybe junior&nbsp;
high or high school. I don't know. I'd have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:52.920 --> 01:24:57.660
think about this. But something like: "Question&nbsp;
your truth," because this is the issue that we're&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:57.660 --> 01:25:04.080
always in is I've been fascinated by this issue.&nbsp;
So, the brain, locked in silence and darkness,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:04.080 --> 01:25:08.340
comes up with an internal model of the world&nbsp;
with all of its truths, its political truths,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:08.340 --> 01:25:13.140
all that stuff. And the funny part is that&nbsp;
when you look at someone else, let's say on&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:13.140 --> 01:25:17.520
the opposite side of the political spectrum from&nbsp;
wherever you are, you think, "Oh, that person's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:17.520 --> 01:25:21.420
misinformed. They're a troll. If I just talked to&nbsp;
them long enough, they'd come to agree with me,”&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:21.420 --> 01:25:25.620
and so on, and it's weird. Everybody believes&nbsp;
this, and everyone on all sides of the spectrum&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:25.620 --> 01:25:30.060
thinks, "Okay, I clearly see the truth, and I&nbsp;
don't know why those other people don't." And so&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:30.060 --> 01:25:35.040
I've just been fascinated by this issue about how&nbsp;
completely we believe our internal models. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:36.120 --> 01:25:42.300
that's what I would try to do with young people&nbsp;
anyway is just get them to question this issue&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:43.380 --> 01:25:47.760
to minimize their high-conviction statements.
Tim Ferriss: So, how would you — let's just&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:47.760 --> 01:25:55.740
use the elementary school or high school example.&nbsp;
This applies to more than that, but let's just say&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:55.740 --> 01:26:02.400
in this case, how would you try to frame this or&nbsp;
spin it so that there's some self-interest, right?&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:03.360 --> 01:26:11.700
In terms of not just the, maybe more general,&nbsp;
become a better thinker, a better human,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:12.300 --> 01:26:16.620
a better member of society, is&nbsp;
there a way that you could position&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:17.820 --> 01:26:27.720
that cross-examination of one's own thoughts and&nbsp;
beliefs in maybe crasser way for self-interest?&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:27.720 --> 01:26:30.900
Just to get more people to do it, right?
David Eagleman: Okay, I don't know if this&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:30.900 --> 01:26:35.220
is going to answer the question directly,&nbsp;
but one way to get people there is to,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:36.360 --> 01:26:41.760
for example, if you admire Sherlock Holmes,&nbsp;
or if a kid watches the Benedict Cumberbatch&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:41.760 --> 01:26:46.140
version or whatever, you think, "Wow, that&nbsp;
guy is really noticing things," and that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:46.140 --> 01:26:49.860
when you start realizing the limits of your&nbsp;
own internal model, and I think, "I would've&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:49.860 --> 01:26:54.060
never noticed that." Why? Because I didn't pay&nbsp;
attention to that. I didn't care about that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:54.060 --> 01:27:00.360
But if you admire people who can do things&nbsp;
and solve things, then I think it's in your&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:00.360 --> 01:27:05.220
self-interest to say, "I want to expand my&nbsp;
internal model. I'm not satisfied to say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:05.220 --> 01:27:10.320
'Okay, I've got it. I know the truth. I'm&nbsp;
not going to do anymore work.'" So, something&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:10.320 --> 01:27:14.400
along those lines of inspiring people that way.
Tim Ferriss: Well, I suppose House would fit in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:14.400 --> 01:27:19.140
there, but he's kind of a dick as a character, so&nbsp;
I'm not sure if you want your kids modeling that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:19.140 --> 01:27:25.020
What's the impetus? And I mean, maybe this&nbsp;
is part of the impetus behind the book&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:25.020 --> 01:27:25.630
that you're working on for kids?
David Eagleman: Yeah. Oh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:25.630 --> 01:27:25.689
the one for kids. Is that —
Tim Ferriss: Oh, well, wait,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:25.689 --> 01:27:25.781
maybe — look, you've got so many projects I&nbsp;
can't keep track. Maybe there's another book?&nbsp;

01:27:25.781 --> 01:27:27.360
David Eagleman: The one for kids is done, and&nbsp;
that's off to the publisher. But the one that I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:27.360 --> 01:27:31.080
working on, the big one I'm working on is called&nbsp;
Empire of the Invisible, and it's going to take me&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:31.080 --> 01:27:33.344
another few years to finish this, but it is —
Tim Ferriss: Nice title.&nbsp;

01:27:33.344 --> 01:27:38.280
David Eagleman: Thank you. It's all about this&nbsp;
issue of all the stuff we don't see and don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:38.280 --> 01:27:43.200
understand. So, I start off with some examples, we&nbsp;
talked about with the electromagnetic spectrum or&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:43.200 --> 01:27:48.600
all the light you don't see but forget it. It's&nbsp;
all about the stuff that you think your model&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:48.600 --> 01:27:54.720
is complete and everything out there to know. But&nbsp;
