01:27:54.720 --> 01:27:59.940
village where you just see the facades of things.&nbsp;
And if I scratch the surface on any — if I were&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:59.940 --> 01:28:03.540
to ask you, "Hey, do you know how the electoral&nbsp;
college works?" You'd say, "Yeah." And I'd say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:03.540 --> 01:28:05.324
"Okay. Can you explain it to me?" And as soon —
Tim Ferriss: And I'd say no.&nbsp;

01:28:05.324 --> 01:28:08.340
David Eagleman: Yeah, exactly. So, as soon&nbsp;
you start scratching the surface on things,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:08.340 --> 01:28:12.420
most people are like, "Oh, I thought I&nbsp;
knew, but maybe I don't really know." So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:12.420 --> 01:28:17.640
that's what it's about, and how we can expand&nbsp;
our perception by asking questions all the time&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:17.640 --> 01:28:22.680
by not taking our truths so seriously,&nbsp;
by understanding there's more out there.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:23.280 --> 01:28:27.480
That's what it's about, and I'm really&nbsp;
jazzed about this book. It's right now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:27.480 --> 01:28:31.620
it's already 120,000 words. And so, I've got&nbsp;
to — as I'm finishing it, I got to really&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:31.620 --> 01:28:35.580
crunch it down so that any human will read it.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, so, for people who don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:36.180 --> 01:28:42.360
make any sense of word counts, I mean, I'd say a&nbsp;
lot of books that get published are around 50,000,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:42.360 --> 01:28:46.800
60,000, something like that, so&nbsp;
you're punching above your weight.&nbsp;

01:28:46.800 --> 01:28:50.340
David Eagleman: 75 is my typical — yeah. Exactly.
Tim Ferriss: 75. How many words was Sum?&nbsp;

01:28:50.340 --> 01:28:52.886
David Eagleman: Oh, that was like 30,000.
Tim Ferriss: Very, yeah. Much shorter.&nbsp;

01:28:52.886 --> 01:28:56.760
David Eagleman: Yeah.
Tim Ferriss: I wonder if — just to bring&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:56.760 --> 01:29:05.880
up the term that one cannot escape these days,&nbsp;
but I wonder if AI, in the cultural conversation&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:05.880 --> 01:29:15.780
context, as people learn more about AI's plural&nbsp;
and large language models and training data, if&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:16.920 --> 01:29:23.040
more people will become aware of their own blind&nbsp;
spots, since we all have our own training data,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:23.640 --> 01:29:25.187
right?
David Eagleman: Yeah.&nbsp;

01:29:25.187 --> 01:29:30.480
Tim Ferriss: I wonder if as more&nbsp;
of these topics become mainstream,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:31.680 --> 01:29:39.180
find their way into scripted television and so&nbsp;
on, if people will become more aware of their&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:39.180 --> 01:29:43.124
own limitations. I mean, that would be a lofty&nbsp;
hope. Not sure if that's going to be the case.&nbsp;

01:29:43.124 --> 01:29:47.580
David Eagleman: I love it. I'm not sure. I'm not&nbsp;
sure I see how though because we're still stuck&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:47.580 --> 01:29:52.440
in our internal models. So, even if I'm talking&nbsp;
to ChatGPT, and it tells me all kinds of things&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:52.440 --> 01:29:56.700
I didn't know, now, I know those. Each time I&nbsp;
add something new to my internal model I think,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:56.700 --> 01:29:59.940
"Okay, now I'm complete. I didn't know&nbsp;
that. Now, I know. Great. I've got it now."&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:01.320 --> 01:30:03.660
So I'm not sure how —
Tim Ferriss: Well, what I mean by that is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:03.660 --> 01:30:09.660
for instance, we've been experimenting on my team&nbsp;
with summarizing past episodes using ChatGPT,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:09.660 --> 01:30:15.720
which I think is an incredible tool. But&nbsp;
what happens in some instances, is it the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:17.220 --> 01:30:21.780
chat interface where I should say the backend&nbsp;
won't interact just with the training data?&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:21.780 --> 01:30:27.000
Because I'm curious to see how it behaves with&nbsp;
different parameters. It won't base its summary&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:27.000 --> 01:30:32.820
strictly on the transcript that's available&nbsp;
on my website, it will also pull from, say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:32.820 --> 01:30:39.960
Google, but much of what has been translated&nbsp;
or interpreted or even summarized elsewhere&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:39.960 --> 01:30:43.800
is completely inaccurate, right? So, I'll go&nbsp;
through, or a team member will go through,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:43.800 --> 01:30:47.340
and they'll find all these mistakes, and&nbsp;
it's like, "Okay, interesting." If we're&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:47.340 --> 01:30:54.300
looking at this as all-seeing, all-knowing sort&nbsp;
of monolith of tech that provides us with answers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:55.200 --> 01:31:04.920
we have a lot of problems to solve. It's been&nbsp;
a useful tool for me to explore the limitations&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:04.920 --> 01:31:10.800
of sets of training data, but maybe I wouldn't&nbsp;
expect it to have that effect across the board.&nbsp;

01:31:10.800 --> 01:31:15.480
David Eagleman: Well, remember, it's not&nbsp;
all-seeing, all-knowing in terms of knowing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:15.480 --> 01:31:20.040
the truth. All it is its read everything,&nbsp;
and it's giving you a summary of everything,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:20.040 --> 01:31:20.791
so if there's —
Tim Ferriss: Totally.&nbsp;

01:31:20.791 --> 01:31:22.860
David Eagleman: If there are people&nbsp;
with different interpretations of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:22.860 --> 01:31:28.380
what you talked about, that's what it'll know.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah. But it'll serve it up as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:28.920 --> 01:31:32.220
summary. But we don't need to belabor&nbsp;
the point. It's just that I've found&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:32.220 --> 01:31:35.700
it to be an interesting exercise.
So let me ask this, and this is in some&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:35.700 --> 01:31:40.260
exploratory bullets that I have listed in front&nbsp;
of me that I was hoping to get to. So, the one&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:40.260 --> 01:31:47.340
that I would love to talk about before we start&nbsp;
to wrap is how to make it seem as though you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:47.340 --> 01:31:54.660
living longer. And perhaps you could provide&nbsp;
a little connective tissue to this in context&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:55.500 --> 01:32:00.300
in reading about your history, the story of the&nbsp;
construction site, when you were eight years old,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:00.300 --> 01:32:07.980
also, I thought gave a very interesting case&nbsp;
study perhaps of how perception of time can differ&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:07.980 --> 01:32:13.121
depending on what you're experiencing. But where&nbsp;
do you think it would make sense to take this?&nbsp;

01:32:13.121 --> 01:32:16.440
David Eagleman: So when I was a kid, I&nbsp;
fell off of this house under construction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:17.580 --> 01:32:22.200
and I fell 12 feet, and it seemed to take a long&nbsp;
time. And I got really interested in this issue&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:22.200 --> 01:32:27.120
of does time run in slow motion when you're in&nbsp;
fear for your life? I mean, I got interested&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:27.120 --> 01:32:31.200
in that question once I became a neuroscientist&nbsp;
and had grown up. And so, I did this experiment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:31.200 --> 01:32:36.660
I think you know about this where I dropped&nbsp;
people from a 150-foot tall tower in free fall,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:36.660 --> 01:32:42.180
falling backwards, and they're caught in a&nbsp;
net below. And I measured the speed of their&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:42.180 --> 01:32:47.880
perception as they're falling because everybody&nbsp;
either has this experience or knows somebody with&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:47.880 --> 01:32:51.660
this experience where they get in a car accident&nbsp;
and things seem to run in slow motion, and they&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:51.660 --> 01:32:58.200
can report all these details of what happened.
Anyway, long story short, it turns out that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:58.200 --> 01:33:02.580
whole thing is a trick of memory. People don't&nbsp;
actually perceive faster during a scary event.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:02.580 --> 01:33:07.080
It's just that they laid down more memory.&nbsp;
So, when they read this back out, they say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:07.080 --> 01:33:12.000
"What just happened? What just happened?" It seems&nbsp;
like they've got much more memory than they would&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:12.000 --> 01:33:18.060
in normal circumstances. So, they say, "I watched&nbsp;
the hood crumple and the rearview mirror fall off,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:18.060 --> 01:33:22.140
and the expression on the other guy's face, and&nbsp;
then," blah, blah, blah. Okay. So anyway, that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:22.140 --> 01:33:28.980
got me thinking about this issue about how we lay&nbsp;
down memory. And it turns out that we touched on&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:28.980 --> 01:33:34.980
memory a while ago, but most things in your life&nbsp;
you do not remember. The number of cracks in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:34.980 --> 01:33:39.840
sidewalk, and who was wearing what, and what was&nbsp;
on the menu on the wall that you — we just don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:39.840 --> 01:33:45.960
remember almost everything because the job of&nbsp;
memory is to write down stuff that matters, that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:45.960 --> 01:33:50.940
emotionally salient or informationally relevant.
And so, you're just writing this tiny fraction.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:50.940 --> 01:33:56.040
But anyway, when you're estimating&nbsp;
how long it's been since some event,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:56.940 --> 01:34:00.840
the way you estimate that is by drawing on the&nbsp;
footage that you have saying, "Oh, well see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:00.840 --> 01:34:03.960
this happened, this happened this…" Okay, so that&nbsp;
must have been two months since I saw my friend,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:03.960 --> 01:34:07.980
or it must have been two years since I&nbsp;
saw this other person, that kind of thing.&nbsp;

01:34:07.980 --> 01:34:13.920
So, anyway, this was a whole bunch of&nbsp;
research in my lab over the course of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:13.920 --> 01:34:20.460
a decade that came together. But the point&nbsp;
is, if you're laying down richer memories,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:21.300 --> 01:34:25.800
you won't necessarily live longer, but you will&nbsp;
make it seem as though you've lived longer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:25.800 --> 01:34:31.080
Because when you're asking what just happened&nbsp;
or how long has it been, you've got more data,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:31.080 --> 01:34:35.340
more footage to draw on. And of course,&nbsp;
we all know this feeling when you go on&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:36.000 --> 01:34:41.220
some super exciting trip on the weekend and you&nbsp;
come back and you're back on a Monday, you think,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:41.220 --> 01:34:45.060
"Oh my gosh, it's been forever since it was&nbsp;
Friday. There's so many things that happened." But&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:45.060 --> 01:34:50.100
if you just have a normal weekend, you think, 'Oh&nbsp;
my gosh, it's Monday. It was just Friday." There's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:50.100 --> 01:34:54.780
nothing to write down, and so you don't feel&nbsp;
that there was much duration that happened there.&nbsp;

01:34:56.100 --> 01:35:02.160
So, this is why I think one of the most important&nbsp;
things in life is about seeking novelty and always&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:02.160 --> 01:35:07.860
putting yourself in new situations such that&nbsp;
you're laying down dense memories. The analogy&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:07.860 --> 01:35:12.060
last thing I'll say on this is when you get to&nbsp;
the end of a childhood summer, it seems that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:12.060 --> 01:35:15.720
the summer lasted forever, and it's because&nbsp;
everything is new. So, you're writing stuff&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:15.720 --> 01:35:21.060
down in your memory. But when you're older, you've&nbsp;
seen it all before, and you get to the end of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:21.060 --> 01:35:24.420
summer and you think, "Wow. I can't believe it's&nbsp;
over already. I don't really remember it at all.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:24.420 --> 01:35:31.500
It seems as though the summer didn't last long."
Tim Ferriss: So, is it fair to then take from that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:31.500 --> 01:35:35.867
the key criterion for rich memory is novelty?
David Eagleman: Yeah.&nbsp;

01:35:35.867 --> 01:35:40.440
Tim Ferriss: Doing new things?
David Eagleman: Exactly. And by the way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:40.440 --> 01:35:46.440
this is one of the tiny silver linings to the&nbsp;
pandemic, which otherwise sucked. But the one&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:46.440 --> 01:35:51.360
interesting part from the point of view of brain&nbsp;
plasticity was that it knocked all of us off our&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:51.360 --> 01:35:58.200
hamster wheels, and we were finding that the world&nbsp;
wasn't what we expected. It was just unbelievable.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:58.200 --> 01:36:02.040
If we had talked in 2019 and said, "Hey, could&nbsp;
this ever happen?" We'd say, "That's a great&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:02.040 --> 01:36:06.120
sci-fi story, but there's no way that it would&nbsp;
be that weird." But it was actually that weird,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:06.120 --> 01:36:10.620
and we all locked ourselves in our houses for&nbsp;
10,000 years. And so this is the kind of thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:12.060 --> 01:36:16.620
when you go to the store and there's no&nbsp;
toilet paper there on the shelves, it takes&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:16.620 --> 01:36:20.160
your internal model and it totally says, "Whoa,&nbsp;
I didn't even know that could happen." You start&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:20.160 --> 01:36:23.880
thinking about supply chains. You start thinking&nbsp;
about how the world works. You're thinking about,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:23.880 --> 01:36:28.920
"Wait, is the barbershop going to be open?&nbsp;
Can I get a haircut during pandemic? Is this&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:28.920 --> 01:36:33.360
coffee shop going to be open?" I mean, it just&nbsp;
forced us to think about the world completely&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:33.360 --> 01:36:39.780
new. And I think if 2019 had continued and it had&nbsp;
just been the same old world, we'd actually be&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:40.380 --> 01:36:46.320
less smart in a sense because our internal models&nbsp;
just would've never gotten challenged in that way.&nbsp;

01:36:47.700 --> 01:36:52.500
Here's my idiothisis, which is an idiotic&nbsp;
hypothesis. I think that — I come up with&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:52.500 --> 01:36:57.420
this a lot, but I think that we might actually see&nbsp;
a tiny decline in dementia some decades from now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:58.080 --> 01:37:04.800
just because we had this opportunity in the middle&nbsp;
of our lives to actually restructure everything&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:04.800 --> 01:37:08.984
and have to rethink everything going on.
Tim Ferriss: Did you say idiothisis?&nbsp;

01:37:08.984 --> 01:37:12.780
David Eagleman: Yeah.
Tim Ferriss: So what other idiothises&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:12.780 --> 01:37:17.460
do you have? Are there any that come to mind?
David Eagleman: Two a day at least. Yeah. Just&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:17.460 --> 01:37:22.680
really, really stupid hypotheses that might just&nbsp;
be right, but they could just as well be wrong.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:22.680 --> 01:37:27.960
Maybe I would define an idiothisis as the kind&nbsp;
of hypothesis where I'm not really attached to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:27.960 --> 01:37:33.180
it enough to fight and die for it, but it's&nbsp;
interesting enough that I'm going to keep&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:33.180 --> 01:37:38.520
an eye on it and see if I can collect data on it.
Tim Ferriss: I love this term, and I love the idea&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:38.520 --> 01:37:46.500
of sharing some of them because I imagine that you&nbsp;
might come up with a lot of hypotheses that might&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:46.500 --> 01:37:53.100
be, in some fashion, worth examining because&nbsp;
you're also not applying pressure to yourself&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:54.120 --> 01:37:58.260
to identify all the scaffolding,&nbsp;
apply all the constraints and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:58.260 --> 01:38:06.180
filters and so on. Okay. Idiothisis. I'm into it.
